\par\par \b \uc2 HK Regulator Sees No Unusual  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Positions By Local Bks  \b0\par\par\uc2 386 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 2003\par 12:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Hong Kong's banking regulator said Wednesday that it hadn't detected any major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation by local banks, despite the recent unusual strengthening of the Hong Kong dollar against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Though the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is formally pegged at a rate of 7.80 to the U.S. dollar, it has been trading above that in the spot market since Sept. 22, thanks to widespread selling of the U.S. dollar and the fact that Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board protects only against depreciation, not appreciation, of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "There's no strong position-taking going on that we would be able to see," said William Ryback, the newly arrived deputy chief executive of the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Banks' positions in the U.S. dollar aren't large enough to cause problems, he added.  \par \par \uc2 "They are managing their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions how I would call normally. They're not taking more risk," Ryback said.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA runs the territory's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board and also supervises the banking sector. Hong Kong banks have traditionally been unwilling to speculate in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for fear of antagonizing the regulator, usually leaving it to overseas banks to take major positions.  \par \par \uc2 "We aren't out there every day looking at banks' position taking," Ryback said, but added that it is unlikely that anything major would go on without the HKMA knowing about it given the "constant contact" it has with banks.  \par \par \uc2 Ryback said the HKMA has long been working on ways to help banks measure the risk of their foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings. "We do want to make sure banks have an ongoing stress test and that we know what those results are."  \par \par \uc2 He declined to comment on the HKMA's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar from strengthening beyond 7.70 to the U.S. dollar, saying his primary responsibility is bank supervision.  \par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar was trading around 7.7300 to the U.S. dollar late Wednesday, with the one-year forward contract at a discount of 220 to 200 pips to the spot rate.  \par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Batson, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Viljoen  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031008dza8000o2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
