\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  OPTIONS-HK dollar bid, volatility still high.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2003\par 07:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 7 (Reuters) - Two weeks after the sudden upward move in the Hong Kong dollar, options traders are not sure which way the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will move next and implied volatility on the peg is near historic highs.   \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar's sudden rise on September 22, after a Group of Seven communique on the weekend caused the yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to spurt, caught markets unawares.   \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been pegged to the U.S. dollar for two decades and had spent three years around 7.8 per U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2  Traders who had positions in the forwards and options markets betting on a devaluation of the Hong Kong dollar have been unwinding ever since, but many of those positions still remain.   \par \par \uc2  "Everyone's trying to sell out their puts and buy the calls, buy at-the-monies and vols," said one options trader, referring to the demand in the past two weeks to exit options to sell the Hong Kong dollar and in some cases reverse bets on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  Forwards have been negative for more than a month, showing a rising premium on the HK dollar.   \par \par \uc2  Implied volatility, a measure of expected volatility priced into options, was quoted near three percent for one-year options last week, which traders said was a historic high.   \par \par \uc2  A few players were buying the HK dollar through options and forwards, but these trades seemed to be an inexpensive way of betting on the Chinese yuan revaluation and rises in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said.   \par \par \uc2  "It is fundamentally stupid. No one really believes Hong Kong should revalue. It has massive structural problems," said James Malcolm, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with JP Morgan.   \par \par \uc2  NO CAP ON HK DOLLAR   \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying U.S. dollars in the market, but analysts say it is not obliged to do so under the guidelines of the Linked Exchange Rate System.   \par \par \uc2  "HKMA has a commitment to sell (U.S.) dollars whenever spot goes to 7.80, but below 7.8 they have latitude in terms of deciding whether or not to buy dollars," said Malcolm.   \par \par \uc2  Traders were wary of positioning for a retreat in the HK dollar back to its 7.8 peg level "because they don't know where the floor is for the spot," he said.   \par \par \uc2  The premium on the HK dollar in forwards, which implies that a forward contract to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earns an attractive interest differential, should normally encourage traders to position for an eventual decline in the HK dollar to 7.8.   \par \par \uc2  "Right now no one will take that risk because people are worried that spot (U.S. dollar) will go a lot lower before it goes higher," Malcolm said.   \par \par \uc2  "What started off as constructive ambiguity has begun to make people very nervous," he said.   \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the risks for an even sharper move up in the Hong Kong dollar were rising with the rate having strayed so far from the peg and remained there for over two weeks.   \par \par \uc2  Hedge funds with loss-making positions would soon find their stop-losses triggered and would rush to buy the Hong Kong dollar, analysts said.   \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the premium on the yuan in non-deliverable forwards was around record highs. One-year NDFs were quoting around 4,500 points, pricing in a nearly six percent appreciation in the yuan in a year.   \par \par \uc2  One-year implied volatility on the yuan was quoted at 9.0/11.5 percent, a historic high.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031007dza7000ni\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
