\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Oct 3  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,881 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2003\par 11:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Economy Minister said Friday that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moves won't have a big negative effect on the economy over the next 6-12 months, but the impact of the yen's rise on exporters needs to be closely watched. ("Japan Takenaka -2: Concerned About \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Impact On Exporters" first take ran at 0023 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The U.S. has abandoned its strong-dollar policy in a dangerous, political ploy aimed at stemming criticism of the Bush administration from Congress and U.S. manufacturers, prominent U.S. investment adviser Richard Medley said Friday. ("=INTERVIEW/Medley -2: Dubai G7 A "Holiday Inn" first take ran at 0814 GMT)  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: The dollar sank to a fresh record discount to the yuan's spot price in the forward market Friday, indicating the market expects China to let its fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  climb 5.1% over the next year. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: China NDFs Point To \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Policy Change From April" ran at 0404 GMT)  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2 : The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar market for the fourth time in two weeks to stem upward pressure on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The quasi-\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold HK$772 million late afternoon when the U.S. dollar fell to around HK$7.7197. The spot rate rebounded slightly after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and was quoted at around HK$7.7195 at 0850 GMT. ("HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -2: Effect Likely Limited" first take ran at 0856 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the Federal Reserve Bank of New York Tuesday was one of many policy options, Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Friday. ("Japan Tanigaki -2: After Yen-Selling Via NY Fed Tuesday" ran at 0030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN CREDIT: Investor demand for high-grade Japanese corporate bonds will remain intact when issuers return to the market in the second fiscal half, but low-grade corporates might find it easier to raise funds through bank loans, analysts say. ("=JAPAN CREDIT: High-Grade Corp Bonds To Stay In Favor 2H" ran at 0127 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese electric power companies are returning to the corporate bond market, having steered clear of the volatile market for almost four months. ("WRAP: Japan Utility Bonds Reappearing After 4 Mo Absence" expected to run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesian Satellite Corp. will increase the size of its bond issue by 750 billion rupiah to IDR2.5 trillion if it gets approval from the capital market regulator, a banker familiar with the deal said Friday. ("Indonesia's Indosat To Upsize Bond To IDR2.5 Tln" expected to run after 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong: The declining trend in the value of retail sales is expected to have stopped in August due to a surge in tourist arrivals and a gradual recovery in local economic sentiment, according to analysts polled ahead of Monday's retail sales data release. The improving trend of retail sales is expected to persist. ("PREVIEW" expected to run by 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: In a sign of a growing rift among property developers, the chairman of the Real Estate Developers Association, Stanley Ho, will write to Chief Executive Tung Chee-hwa urging the suspension of land sales in Hong Kong be extended for another year. ("HK Ppty Assoc Calls For Land Sale Suspension Till End '04" ran at 0515 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves hit a record high of US$190.58 billion at the end of September, the island's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday. ("Taiwan Sep/\b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Reserves-2: Euro,Yen Appreciation Vs US$" first take ran at 0825 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: Declining shipments of electronics and semiconductor products dragged Philippine exports down for the second month running in August - bleak news for the export-driven Southeast Asian economy Friday. ("=WRAP: Philippine Aug Exports Down But Recovery In Sight" ran at 0712 GMT.)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: The spreads between India's corporate and federal debt markets should compress in the coming weeks as yields on the former soften to adjust to a recent slide in government bond counterparts, says ICICI Securities' Vice President Piyush Garg. ("=INTERVIEW: India Corp Bond Yield Spreads Set To Narrow" ran at 0854 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's Ministry of Agriculture and Forestry said Friday that suspected cases of a wasting syndrome in pigs will have little if any effect on the country's international trade. ("NZ Ministry Says Suspected Pig Disease Won't Affect Trade" ran at 0242 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Most New Zealanders oppose visits by nuclear ships, even as part of the global war on terrorism, a poll released Friday showed. ("Nuclear Ships Still Unwelcome In NZ, Even To Fight Terror" ran at 2255 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Small businesses continued to inject life into the New Zealand economy in the 12 months to June, although they feel increasingly burdened by government rules and regulations, according to a survey released Friday. ("NZ Small Businesses Spur Econ Growth Despite Rules Burden" ran at 1300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's manufacturing sector continued to expand in September, with the pace picking up slightly from the previous month on the back of an increase in news orders. The Purchasing Managers' Index was at 52% in September, up 0.7 point from 51.3% in August, marking the fourth consecutive month of expansion. (Ran at 1430 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HANOI: Vietnam's tourism sector continues to show signs of recovery from the effects of SARS, with a 8.6% on-month increase in international visitor arrivals in September to 210,091, a government report issued Friday shows. ("Vietnam Sep Visitors +8.6% Vs Aug; Jan-Sep -17.3% On Yr" ran at 0338 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar was stuck in a narrow range against the yen and the euro in Asia Friday as market players remained preoccupied with the upcoming release of key U.S. employment data later in the global session. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0536 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    EQUITIES \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO: Mitsubishi Motors Corp. said Friday it will initially invest more than Y10 billion to build a domestic production line for a new series of car engines jointly developed by DaimlerChrysler AG and Hyundai Motor Co. ("Mitsubishi Motors -2: Production To Begin In Spring 2005" first take ran at 0719 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO: Memory-Tech Corp., a Japanese producer of optical discs, said Friday it has developed a technology to cheaply mass-produce optical discs that use a next-generation standard promoted by Toshiba Corp. and NEC Corp. ("Japan Co Develops Tech For NEC/Toshiba-Type Optical Discs" ran at 0329 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE: Singapore Technologies Pte. Ltd. Friday confirmed that it's raising S$801 million through an issue of seven-year bonds that are convertible into shares of listed CapitaLand Ltd. and ST Engineering Ltd. ("Singapore Technologies -2: Seven-Year Bond Issue" first take ran at 0652 GMT; "WRAP" expected to run by 1000 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's four largest makers of dynamic random access memory chips have decided to delay indefinitely asking their government to take trade action against South Korea's Hynix Semiconductor Inc. ("Taiwan DRAM Makers Step Back From Action Vs Korea's Hynix" first take ran at 0427 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 =ASIA IPO FOCUS: C.P. Seven-Eleven PCL, Thailand's largest chain of convenience stores, is well on track for a successful debut in the Stock Exchange of Thailand given its well-known brand name and strong foothold in the retail sector. ("=Asia IPO Focus: Thai 7-Eleven IPO Success Around Corner" ran at 0613 GMT; "Thai C.P 7-Eleven Keen On Getting China Operating Rights" ran at 0648 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO: Parcel delivery service company Yamato Transport Co. has launched a wholly-owned subsidiary in Shanghai to deliver small-lot cargo from China to customers in Japan, a Yamato spokeswoman said Friday. ("Yamato Transport Starts China Parcel Delivery Svc Unit" ran at 0225 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HONG KONG: Two weeks ago, Hong Kong-listed container firm COSCO Pacific Ltd. bought a stake its parent's logistics business, promising the new division will be one of its three core \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  within five years. ("=HK COSCO Pacific's Logistic Move Creates Few Synergies" ran at 0526 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MELBOURNE: Australia's CSL Ltd. said Friday it intends to look at any rival blood plasma production assets put on the auction block by German healthcare giant Bayer AG. ("=WRAP: Baxter Assets Raise Interest Of Australia's CSL" ran at 0716 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MELBOURNE: Australia's Telstra Corp. fleshed out details of its A$800 million to A$1 billion share buyback Friday but warned of flat revenue growth, suggesting the industry remains in the doldrums. ("=WRAP: Australia's Telstra Buyback Invigorates Shares" ran at 0701 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE: Singapore Telecommunications Ltd. will make a profitable exit from Europe when Belgian operator Belgacom SA is floated next year. ("=WRAP:SingTel Bows Out Of Belgacom, Europe With Fat Pocket" ran at 0645 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TAIPEI: SinoPac Holdings Co. said Friday it was open to an acquisition by another bank and expected to select an investment bank as a merger-and-acquisition adviser by the end of the year. ("Taiwan SinoPac Hldgs Open To Acquisition By Another Bank" ran at 0420 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO: South Korea's petroleum importers are finding it tough going to hang on to their share of the retail gasoline and diesel market, and the introduction of higher taxes will further increase the pressure on them.("=INTERVIEW:S Korea Oil Importers Squeezed By Higher Taxes" ran at 0430 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO: Japan and Russia plan a fourth round of talks in late October to discuss a Japan-backed project to build an oil pipeline running from eastern Siberia to Russia's Pacific coast. ("Japan, Russia To Hold 4th Round Of Siberia Oil Pipe Talks" to run after 0930 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE: The potential impact that the European Commission's ban on single-hull tankers at European ports and offshore installations could have on the west-east fuel oil arbitrage flow remains unclear, brokers said Friday. How will arbitrage traders work around the single hull tanker ban being introduced in the E.U. from Oct. 21. ("Impact Of EC Single-Hull Tanker Ban On Rates Unclear" ran at 0756 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE: Mideast crudes premiums are poised to rise as recent flurry of arbitrage shipments from West Africa and Europe may be slowing and buyers are expected to resume purchases of Gulf grades. ("=OUTLOOK: Good Demand Prospects For Gulf, Asian Crudes" ran at 0808 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE: Crude-driven gains in most Asian oil product prices will likely be trimmed in the week ahead as regional fundamentals are lagging recent price increases. ("=OUTLOOK: Asian Oil Pdt Cracks To Narrow; Saudi Term Eyed" ran at 0834 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CANBERRA: Having taken almost a decade to settle in, Australia's Sino Gold Ltd. now plans to expand \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as China's only foreign producer of gold, said Chief Executive Jake Klein. ("INTERVIEW: Australia Sino Gold Poised For Growth In China" ran at 0824 GMT)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031003dza3000bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
