\par\par \b \uc2 HK Dlr Mkts Late: Speculation Widens Discount On HKD Fwds  \b0\par\par\uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2003\par 10:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The discount on Hong Kong dollar forwards widened sharply Thursday, amid renewed speculation that China and Hong Kong may in future release their grip on their respective fixed exchange rates and let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark one-year forward was trading at 140 to 130 below the spot rate late Thursday, compared with a discount of 85 to 75 on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The market was closed Wednesday for the National Day holiday.  \par \par \uc2 After trading at a premium to the spot rate for the last couple of years, forwards suddenly switched to a discount last week as traders unwound long-standing positions that hedged against the depreciation of the Hong Kong dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The sharper discount in Hong Kong dollar forwards was linked to similar moves in the offshore nondeliverable forward market for the Chinese yuan, where the one-year forwards were quoted at a discount of around 3850 to the spot rate Thursday, compared with a discount of 3500 to 3600 last week.  \par \par \uc2 Though it wasn't clear what sparked the renewed speculation, traders said the discount on yuan nondeliverable forwards is now likely to test 4000 in the next few days. "It won't go much below that. It's a critical psychological level," said one trader.  \par \par \uc2 That in turn could push the discount on the one-year Hong Kong dollar forward below 200, another trader said, arguing that fund flows into Hong Kong and the U.S. dollar's weakness against the yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are also contributing factors.  \par \par \uc2 The spot exchange rate for the Hong Kong dollar was also under pressure Thursday, with the U.S. dollar/Hong Kong dollar rate falling to 7.7375 late Thursday compared with 7.7440 Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 However, the rate rebounded from an intraday low of around HK$7.7335 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell HK$387 million to prevent its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising further against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts had expected such \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the spot rate diverged from the official peg rate of 7.80 last week, though it is still unclear how long it will take to get back to return to that level.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a matter of when people get tired of speculating on appreciation," one trader said. "People really don't think the Hong Kong government will change the peg."  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong interbank offered rates, already lower early in the day due to the falling forwards, declined further after the HKMA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed more funds into the market. The one-month Hibor was quoted at 0.50% compared with 0.62% Tuesday, and the one-year Hibor was at 1.03% as against 1.18% previously.  \par \par \uc2 "We think Hibors of three-month and shorter tenors could decline further to levels closer to zero in the coming weeks," Citigroup economist Joe Lo wrote in a report this week, citing fund inflows and more expected \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the HKMA.  \par \par \uc2 Yields on long-dated \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  exchange fund notes declined, tracking lower yields on the benchmark 10-year U.S. Treasury. The two-year note was trading at 1.26%, compared with 1.40% Tuesday, and the 10-year note was at 4.17%, compared with 4.24% previously.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                   Latest        Late Tuesday \par Overnight Hibor   0.25%         0.60% \par 1-mo Hibor        0.50%         0.62% \par 3-mo Hibor        0.68%         0.81% \par 1-yr Hibor        1.03%         1.18% \par USD/HKD Spot      HK$7.7375     HK$7.7440 \par 6-mo HKD Fwd      -135 to -125  -89 to -84 \par 1-yr HKD Fwd      -140 to -130  -85 to -75 \par  \par 3.73% EF Note Due June 5, 2013: \par  \par    Price: 96.85 Vs 96.37 \par    Yield: 4.17% Vs 4.23% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Andrew Batson and Raymond Tsoi, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; andrew.batson@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by John Viljoen  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1053 4178  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031002dza2000b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
