\par\par \b \uc2 HKMA  \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  In HK Dollar Market, Injects HK$387 Mln  \b0\par\par\uc2 484 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2003\par 09:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar market Thursday selling HK$387 million to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rising further amid speculation of an appreciation against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The quasi-\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported its sales of the Hong Kong dollar when the U.S. dollar/Hong Kong dollar spot rate hit an intraday low of around HK$7.7335.  \par \par \uc2 The spot rate rebounded moderately after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The U.S. dollar was quoted at HK$7.7375 around 0810 GMT, compared with HK$7.7440 Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The market was closed Wednesday for a public holiday.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA also bought about US$60 million last week to calm the effects of market speculation.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  triggered some short-covering of the U.S. dollar, which may keep a lid on the Hong Kong dollar in the near term," one trader said.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the spot rate is expected to remain below the level of HK$7.80 at which the Hong Kong dollar is pegged to the U.S. dollar in coming weeks, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Hong Kong interbank offered rates fell after the injection as the market was flooded with excess funds.  \par \par \uc2 The Overnight Hibor fell to 0.25%, compared with 0.60% Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA is obliged to buy the Hong Kong dollar from banks at the peg rate, which essentially puts a floor on the Hong Kong dollar's level against the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 However the convertibility undertaking is one-sided, which means \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar is at the HKMA's discretion. Therefore, theoretically there is no ceiling for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's value.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed the HKMA isn't willing to tolerate a large deviation from the peg rate, which helped to calm down the market a bit," the trader said. "But, given the strong local equities, there could be more equity-driven fund inflows, keeping upward pressure on the Hong Kong dollar."  \par \par \uc2 The discount on the one-year yuan nondeliverable forward also widened Thursday to around 3800 on renewed speculation the yuan may be allowed to appreciate.  \par \par \uc2 This implies a more than 4% appreciation in the yuan against the U.S. dollar over the next 12 months, traders said. "It may break through 4000 in the next couple of days," one noted.  \par \par \uc2 Others doubted whether such a deep discount is sustainable, but a move to 4000 would almost certainly cause discounts on Hong Kong dollar forwards to widen further.  \par \par \uc2 The one-year Hong Kong dollar forward was quoted at a discount of 140 to 130 late Tuesday, tracking the movement in the spot rate and the yuan NDFs, compared with a discount of 85 to 75 Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 -By Raymond Tsoi, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; raymond.tsoi@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by John Viljoen  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031002dza20009c\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 Brave New World for the Hong Kong Dollar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 685 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2003\par \uc2 PR Newswire Asia\par \uc2 PRNASI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003, PR Newswire  \par \par \uc2 ING Financial Markets recommends long positions in the SAR's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, Sept. 30 /XFNHK/ - ING Financial Markets (ING) today predicted that the Hong Kong Dollar is set to experience a period of buying pressure as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors rebalance their market positions. The improving economic environment and calls from G7 for Asia to allow undervalued exchange rates to appreciate will lead to a scaling back of entrenched long U.S. Dollar, short Hong Kong Dollar positions, ING believes.  \par \par \uc2 ING forecasts that the Hong Kong Dollar one-year forward will see increased buying in the market and will trade to -200 pips from its present level of -60 pips, which would imply a spot exchange rate in one year of HK$7.73 to the U.S. Dollar.  \par \par \uc2 This dramatic shift in investors' positions comes as the long-standing reasons for traditionally selling the Hong Kong Dollar have been replaced by arguments to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The US administration's tacit acceptance of the end of the strong dollar policy has reversed one of the principal arguments for bearishness. The market also is now selling down the U.S. Dollar to buy Yen, as investors sense that the current upswing in the Japanese market may be powerful enough to stem deflation and enable the banks to end their struggle with bad loans. The upward pressure on the Yen has transmitted like pressure to the rest of Asia. In addition, China can also no longer be considered a reason to sell the Hong Kong Dollar. The markets appear to be indicating a four per cent appreciation in the Renminbi over the next twelve months, given the one-year non-deliverable forward price, which currently stands at RMB7.947.  \par \par \uc2 Tim Condon, Chief Economist, Asia stated: "The domestic arguments for a weaker Hong Kong dollar are also receding as people move on from the low-point in March and April of this year and the SARS crisis. We see the domestic economy rebounding through a tourism-led recovery, driving GDP forecasts for 2004 and 2005 upwards, with consumption snapping back as optimism over the future returns. Investors are seeing that there is two-way risk in the Hong Kong Dollar and are looking to scale back their short positions."  \par \par \uc2 ING forecasts that Hong Kong's GDP will reach five per cent in both 2004 and 2005.  \par \par \uc2 The prediction that the Hong Kong Dollar faces upside pressure, rather than only downside pressure, comes as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last week, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  Dollar strengthened to a six-year high of 7.7050 against the U.S. Dollar. ING believes that the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sought to stabilise the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the face of snowballing U.S. Dollar selling pressure. ING foresees that the forward discount on the Hong Kong Dollar price will continue to widen as investors and risk managers rebalance their positions in light their new appreciation of the Hong Kong Dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Contact ING:Sheel Kohli, ING Head of Corporate Communications, Asia, +852-2913-8768,sheel.kohli@asia.ing.com  \par \par \uc2 Note for Editors:ING Financial Markets is a marketing name for ING Bank N.V.'s activities in debt market origination, securitisation and syndicated loans in Asia. ING Bank N.V. is a division of ING Group, one of the largest financial groups in Europe. ING conducts a wide range of international financial services including corporate finance, M&A advisory, origination of capital markets products, securitisation and corporate and structured lending. It also offers, sales, trading and distribution of equity and debt market products, research, treasury, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , money markets and derivatives, cash management and international payments, wholesale insurance and employee benefits.  \par \par \uc2 ING Group is active in insurance, banking and asset management across 15 countries in the Asia Pacific region where it employs over 13,000 people. ING Group employs over 115,000 staff globally.  \par \par \uc2 SOURCE ING Financial Markets  \par \par \uc2 CONTACT:Sheel KohliING Head of Corporate Communications, AsiaTel: +852-2913-8768sheel.kohli@asia.ing.com.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument PRNASI0020031105dz9u000c5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich24\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal360
