\par\par \b \uc2 Brave New World for the Hong Kong Dollar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 662 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2003\par \uc2 Pressi.com\par \uc2 PCOMEN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2003 by  \uc2 www.pressi.com\par \par \uc2 ING Financial Markets recommends long positions in the SAR's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ING Financial Markets (ING) today predicted that the Hong Kong Dollar is set to experience a period of buying pressure as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors rebalance their market positions. The improving economic environment and calls from G7 for Asia to allow undervalued exchange rates to appreciate will lead to a scaling back of entrenched long U.S. Dollar, short Hong Kong Dollar positions, ING believes. ING forecasts that the Hong Kong Dollar one-year forward will see increased buying in the market and will trade to -200 pips from its present level of -60 pips, which would imply a spot exchange rate in one year of HK$7.73 to the U.S. Dollar. This dramatic shift in investors' positions comes as the long-standing reasons for traditionally selling the Hong Kong Dollar have been replaced by arguments to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The US administration's tacit acceptance of the end of the strong dollar policy has reversed one of the principal arguments for bearishness. The market also is now selling down the U.S. Dollar to buy Yen, as investors sense that the current upswing in the Japanese market may be powerful enough to stem deflation and enable the banks to end their struggle with bad loans. The upward pressure on the Yen has transmitted like pressure to the rest of Asia. In addition, China can also no longer be considered a reason to sell the Hong Kong Dollar. The markets appear to be indicating a four per cent appreciation in the Renminbi over the next twelve months, given the one-year non-deliverable forward price, which currently stands at RMB7.947. Tim Condon, Chief Economist, Asia stated: "The domestic arguments for a weaker Hong Kong dollar are also receding as people move on from the low-point in March and April of this year and the SARS crisis. We see the domestic economy rebounding through a tourism-led recovery, driving GDP forecasts for 2004 and 2005 upwards, with consumption snapping back as optimism over the future returns. Investors are seeing that there is two-way risk in the Hong Kong Dollar and are looking to scale back their short positions." ING forecasts that Hong Kong's GDP will reach five per cent in both 2004 and 2005. The prediction that the Hong Kong Dollar faces upside pressure, rather than only downside pressure, comes as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets last week, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  Dollar strengthened to a six-year high of 7.7050 against the U.S. Dollar. ING believes that the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sought to stabilise the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the face of snowballing U.S. Dollar selling pressure. ING foresees that the forward discount on the Hong Kong Dollar price will continue to widen as investors and risk managers rebalance their positions in light their new appreciation of the Hong Kong Dollar. Contact ING: Sheel Kohli, ING Head of Corporate Communications, Asia +852 2913 8768 sheel.kohli@asia.ing.com Note for Editors: ING Financial Markets is a marketing name for ING Bank N.V.'s activities in debt market origination, securitisation and syndicated loans in Asia. ING Bank N.V. is a division of ING Group, one of the largest financial groups in Europe. ING conducts a wide range of international financial services including corporate finance, M&A advisory, origination of capital markets products, securitisation and corporate and structured lending. It also offers, sales, trading and distribution of equity and debt market products, research, treasury, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , money markets and derivatives, cash management and international payments, wholesale insurance and employee benefits. ING Group is active in insurance, banking and asset management across 15 countries in the Asia Pacific region where it employs over 13,000 people. ING Group employs over 115,000 staff globally.  \par \par \uc2 Dokument PCOMEN0020030930dz9u00335\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
