\par\par \b \uc2 HK$ Gain Sign Of Confidence But Appreciation Temporary  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Raymond Tsoi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 811 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2003\par 13:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar's recent surge against the U.S. dollar - the biggest since the 1997 Asian financial crisis - sent traders covering their short positions in panicky trade this week, but their reaction may be overdone.  \par \par \uc2 Many market observers considered the Hong Kong dollar's sharp gains to be no more than a blip as there has been no sudden improvement in the city's economic fundamentals to justify such an appreciation.  \par \par \uc2 Still, many economists argue the rise can be seen as a sign of returning confidence.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar/Hong Kong dollar spot rate plunged in London and New York trade Monday, then staged a partial rebound and stabilized between HK$7.7500 and HK$7.7600, following \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which said it bought US$60 million from the market Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was small but effective in a market that can see hundreds of US$50 million or US$100 million trades.  \par \par \uc2 The HKMA was giving the market a clear signal that it isn't willing to tolerate a sustained deviation from 7.80, the rate at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged to the U.S. dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The recent gains in the Hong Kong dollar are unusual.  \par \par \uc2 "For the last 10 to 12 years we haven't seen such strong appreciative pressure on the Hong Kong dollar, especially in the spot market," Standard Chartered Economist Mike Moran said.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, it was a dramatic reversal from the situation just a few weeks ago when most investors were still hedging against a Hong Kong dollar devaluation on worries over the city's economic weakness.  \par \par \uc2 Economists said the significance of the recent Hong Kong dollar gains is that it showed a recovery of confidence.  \par \par \uc2 "For the first time in years, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is starting to believe that Hong Kong's adjustment may have come to an end, and the Hong Kong dollar may not need to depreciate," said ABN Amro Chief Asian Strategist Eddie Wong.  \par \par \uc2 The new perception of Hong Kong could trigger a sustainable inflow of liquidity back to the city, Wong said.  \par \par \uc2 "This will be hugely positive for Hong Kong, notably in its asset prices, including stocks and property," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, Hong Kong shares recorded their biggest percentage gain in nearly five months Wednesday as the Hang Seng Index rose to a 15-month high of 11295.89 and the share market recorded a trading volume of HK$19.81 billion, its biggest in three years.  \par \par \uc2 Property companies led gainers.  \par \par \uc2 Fundamental Improvements Don't Justify Appreciation  \par \par \uc2 Although most economists agreed the economy is gradually picking up and confidence is being restored, many doubted the economy is strong enough to warrant a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation after 20 years of a pegged exchange rate.  \par \par \uc2 "The domestic fundamentals are improving, but they're not great," Moran said.  \par \par \uc2 Unemployment is at a near-record high and consumer prices have fallen for 58 consecutive months.  \par \par \uc2 "We still see Hong Kong has only recently closed the appreciation (gap) against its trading partners which it suffered during the Asian financial crisis," said Barclays Bank analyst Pieter van der Schaft.  \par \par \uc2 National Australia Bank's economist, Kevin Lai, agreed the Hong Kong dollar's sharp gains won't last.  \par \par \uc2 "The city's economy is still struggling to come out of more than four and a half years of deflation, which reflects a lack of competitiveness," Lai said.  \par \par \uc2 "Yes, overall economic sentiment is improving, but not to the extent of justifying a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Technical factors also played a role, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "The market was overly pessimistic about the Hong Kong dollar previously, (and) many have hedged against or speculated on a devaluation, building up a massive demand to buy back the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the spot rate plunged through all major chart points," said one money trader.  \par \par \uc2 Traders also attributed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength to broad speculation on Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation led by the Chinese yuan, following the Group of Seven's call over the weekend for more flexible exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 "The G-7's call fueled speculation about an appreciation of the yuan. Given the close ties between China and Hong Kong, and the fact that both are linked to the U.S. dollar, the upward pressure spread to the Hong Kong dollar," the trader said.  \par \par \uc2 "But fundamentally, Hong Kong is very different from China, there is no reason why the Hong Kong dollar should appreciate if the yuan is revalued upward," he added.  \par \par \uc2 "You'll find the U.S. dollar/Hong Kong dollar spot rate gradually reverts to the more usual tight ranges," Standard Chartered's Moran said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Raymond Tsoi, Dow Jones Newswires; 852-2802-7002; raymond.tsoi@dowjones.com (John Ryan contributed to this story)  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Andrew Bullard  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030924dz9o000kc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
