\par\par \b \uc2 Asia Econ Growth On Track Even With Stronger  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,104 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2003\par 12:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asia is expected to remain the world's fastest growing region even if the area's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  edge up in value after major industrial powers called over the weekend for freer \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements.  \par \par \uc2 But a sharp drop by the dollar in the wake of the Group of Seven meeting could seriously dent the prospects for a region just emerging from the economic damage of SARS and the Iraq war, economists say.  \par \par \uc2 G7 finance ministers and central bankers, meeting in Dubai over the weekend, called for "more flexibility in exchange rates" to promote "smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system."  \par \par \uc2 This amounts to a challenge to Asian export powerhouses, especially G7 member Japan and China, not to sell their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to foster export-led growth.  \par \par \uc2 The G7's call "is a reflection of confidence that the global (economic) growth is on track," says David Cohen, a regional economist at MMS International in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 But if Asian governments were to let the markets set their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, it would mark quite a change in dearly held policies.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has bought an estimated $100 billion for yen so this year - well above the previous full-year record - while China also regularly must buy dollars to keep the yuan pegged around CNY8.28 to a dollar. Malaysia and \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  also have \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegs, while South Korea and Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep theirs in check.  \par \par \uc2 The result in Asia has been a massive swelling of already huge \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves as national authorities sell their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for dollars. In the U.S., manufacturers and some lawmakers charge that artificially undervalued Asian exchange rates are flooding the world with cheap Asian goods, ramping up the U.S. trade deficit and costing American jobs.  \par \par \uc2 Although Japan agreed to the G7 call for freer \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a G7 finance minister said China didn't object, Tokyo, Beijing, and Seoul made clear Monday they haven't changed their plans to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable to prevent exports from derailing.  \par \par \uc2 But the International Monetary Fund's director for Asia-Pacific, David Burton predicted little economic damage from the dollar's decline.  \par \par \uc2 "Most of these economies are growing robustly, and many of their exchange rates in real effective terms have depreciated somewhat in recent times, so I don't think modest appreciations would have a major effect on them," Burton told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview in Dubai.  \par \par \uc2 In its semiannual World Economic Outlook last week, the IMF forecast Asia excluding Japan would grow 5.9% this year, nearly twice the global average and trimmed hardly changed from the April forecast of 6.0% despite the ravages of SARS. The IMF more than doubled its 2003 growth forecast for Japan to 2% from 0.8%.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par            Bank Of Japan In The Spotlight \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A key issue for Asian economies in the wake of the G7 statement will be whether Tokyo can let the yen rise without roiling the markets or damaging its damaging the economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After a decade of stagnation, Japan's economy grew an 3.9% annualized in the second quarter - the best in the G7.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There is room for the yen to strengthen to Y110-Y115, says Cohen at MMS International. Morgan Stanley equity strategist Naoki Kamiyama says a Y108-Y112 range is still endurable for Japan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar, which had already sagged Y3.5 last week, plunged in early Asian trade Monday to as low as Y111.37 - its lowest level since December 2000 - from Y114.27 late Friday in New York. Around 1000 GMT the dollar was at Y112.34.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some traders said they thought the Bank of Japan was selling yen covertly Monday, but Tokyo appeared to have lowered its dollar target. The Finance Ministry all year has ordered the Bank of Japan to sell yen heavily to defend the dollar at Y115 - a level that finally broke Friday as G7 ministers were arriving in Dubai.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar is now below the Y115 average rate that some exporters, such as Toyota Motor Corp., are counting on for the six-month period to next March. Shares of Japanese exporters tumbled Monday, helping drive the recently rallying Nikkei 225 Stock Average down 4.2%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While growing, the world's second-largest economy is still not out of the woods. Its banking system is choked full of bad debts, while consumers remain reluctant to spend.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par           China Pressured To Break The Peg \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although the G7 didn't name names, it was clearly joining U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow in pushing China to loosen up on the yuan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan is convertible only on the current account, and Beijing has kept it around its current level for five years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speculation regime-change has dogged the dollar for weeks in the market for forward delivery against the yuan, which can be used in place of the spot market to make bets on the yuan's long-term direction. The dollar's one-year discount steepened to a record 2400 points at one stage Monday, implying expectations of the dollar falling nearly 3% to 8.0374 yuan over the next 12 months.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulator insisted again Monday they;re in no change to give up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stability, saying it's an internal matter.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many analysts agree China likely won't budge soon.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The yuan won't see much movement," says Dong Tao, an economist at Credit Suisse First Boston in Hong Kong.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The head of research at a U.S. brokerage said China's exchange rate will remain a "political issue" ahead of the U.S. presidential elections next year, and the question is whether Beijing will be able to withstand the pressure.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An Asian central banker, who declined to be named, said China needs to "do something," but just how and when it does will remain a mystery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What's not a mystery is the Chinese position.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A stable yuan is not only good for us, it's good for our immediate neighbors and for the whole world," deputy governor of the Bank of China, Li Ruogu, said recently.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Li acknowledged China has a big trade surplus with the U.S., but said cheap labor costs - not exchange rates - are the main reason.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're committed to liberalization, but I can't give you a very clear timetable of how long it will take," Li said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     -By Hasan Jafri, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4151; hasan.jafri@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Benjamin Pedley in Dubai and Arran Scott in Tokyo contributed to this item.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020030922dz9m000