\par\par \b \uc2  BOJ Matsushima: Don't Plan To Weaken Yen By  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 425 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2001\par 02:46\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- The Bank of Japan has no plan to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to weaken the yen as a way to ease Japan's debilitating deflationary conditions, a senior BOJ official said Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "We don't have any intention of inducing the yen weaker by maneuvering (or) by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," said BOJ executive director Masayuki Matsushima. \par \par \uc2  In September, the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, selling yen for dollars and euros in a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market incursions that pushed the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above Y119 from Y116. Around 0219 GMT the dollar was trading at Y122.00. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ has no authority to set \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy which is entirely controlled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . However, Matsushima remarks suggest the BOJ remains opposed to using indirect means - such as increasing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's purchases of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated bonds - to cheapen the yen and help alleviate Japan's worsening deflation. \par \par \uc2  In any case, Matsushima said deteriorating economic conditions in Japan suggest the yen is unlikely to naturally appreciate against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , indicating the Japanese monetary authorities may feel comfortable about the current dollar-yen exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese government has not targeted a fixed exchange rate," Matsushima said in a speech to the East Asia Economic Summit in Hong Kong \par \par \uc2  "We think the rather bleak economic prospects (mean) an appreciation of the yen at present is undermined by economic growth. There is no reason for the yen to appreciate," the BOJ official said. \par \par \uc2  In a semiannual report released Monday the BOJ policy board said it expects the economy to shrink by around 1% in the current financial year to next March and for deflation to accelerate. \par \par \uc2  Despite admitting the outlook looks dismal, the BOJ board opted to leave unchanged its current super-easy policy of maintaining private banks' current-account deposits at the BOJ "above Y6 trillion." \par \par \uc2  The shift to a "quantitative easing" policy last March has kept short-term interest rates anchored virtually at zero ever since. \par \par \uc2  Matsushima said the BOJ is resolved to ridding the economy of deflation and will maintain its current policy stance until consumer prices start rising at a rate convincingly above zero. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The BOJ, under the current policy framework, is firmly committed to\par maintain its current extremely lax monetary posture until CPI (the\par consumer price index) ceases to decline," Matsushima said.\par    -By Liz Rudall and Anette Jonsson, Dow Jones Newswires;\par 852-2802-7002; anette.jonssondowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011031dxav0003b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
