\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Markets-Wall St shakes stocks, bonds await Argentina ripple. \b0\par\par\uc2      By Nick Edwards \par \uc2 1,198 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Juni 2001\par 12:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2      HONG KONG, June 19 (Reuters) - Asian asset markets failed \par \par \uc2  to fight through Wall Street's gloom on Tuesday, with most \par \par \uc2  regional equity benchmarks flat to weaker and fixed income \par \par \uc2  markets trading nervously in the shadow cast by Argentina's \par \par \uc2  flailing economy. \par \par \uc2      A fall of two percent on Nasdaq kept Asian stocks bowed, \par \par \uc2  with only Hong Kong and Malaysia strong enough to break \par \par \uc2  decisively into positive territory, and weighed heavily on \par \par \uc2  sentiment for dollar bonds which saw spreads to risk-free U.S. \par \par \uc2  Treasuries widen an average of 10 basis points on the day. \par \par \uc2     Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained hostage to the fortunes of Japan's \par \par \uc2  yen which sank to one month lows against the dollar before \par \par \uc2  recovering sharply in late trade after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor \par \par \uc2  Masaru Hayami said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was necessary if \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates \par \par \uc2  move too rapidly. \par \par \uc2      Tokyo's Nikkei 225 stock index wiped out earlier gains to \par \par \uc2  end lower for the third straight session, losing 0.97 percent \par \par \uc2  after concerns over the financial health of debt-strapped \par \par \uc2  Japanese retailer Mycal Corp chilled sentiment. \par \par \uc2      ASIAN BOND SPREADS WIDEN \par \par \uc2      Asian dollar bond spreads were wider across the board in \par \par \uc2  thin trade with a fall on Wall Street undermining prices more \par \par \uc2  than confusion over a change in Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. \par \par \uc2      Asian bonds have taken it on the chin since Nortel Networks \par \par \uc2  warned of a $19.2 billion second quarter loss last week sending \par \par \uc2  U.S. corporate bond spreads sprawling and pushing Asian \par \par \uc2  investors to lock in profits on benchmark paper that has \par \par \uc2  tightened an average of 30 basis points over the past two \par \par \uc2  weeks. \par \par \uc2      Confusion over Argentina's introduction of a special \par \par \uc2  exchange rate for exporters and importers sent emerging market \par \par \uc2  bond investors in New York scurrying for safety on Monday, but \par \par \uc2  near term fallout for Asian bonds were seen muted. \par \par \uc2      "This may make people realise that there are still risks in \par \par \uc2  the global financial system and people may re-evaluate some of \par \par \uc2  the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia," Brian Verlaan, head of Asian \par \par \uc2  fixed income research at Standard Chartered Bank in Singapore \par \par \uc2  said. \par \par \uc2      "This will ultimately, coupled with some of the economic \par \par \uc2  fundamentals in these countries, cause people to reassess \par \par \uc2  spreads, but I don't think there's an immediate knock-on \par \par \uc2  effect," he told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2      The long end of the Philippine yield curve felt most \par \par \uc2  pressure with the country's 9.875 percent sovereign paper due \par \par \uc2  in 2019 pushing out about five basis points (bps) at the open \par \par \uc2  and moving 10-15 further out to be quoted at about 605 bps over \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasuries in late trade. \par \par \uc2      At the shorter end Philippine '10s were about two bps wider \par \par \uc2  at Asia's open, but had widened a further five bps in late \par \par \uc2  trade to around 497 bps over Treasuries, according to data from \par \par \uc2  Reuters Fixed Income 3000. \par \par \uc2      The sub-investment grade Philippines offers the most \par \par \uc2  representative comparison to Latin American risk and is the \par \par \uc2  biggest Asian component in the benchmark EMBI, making the paper \par \par \uc2  sensitive to moves in the index, dominated by Argentina. \par \par \uc2      PHILIPPINE PESO SLIDES \par \par \uc2      The peso slid to five-month lows of 52.38 as corporates \par \par \uc2  scrambled to buy the dollar on the weaker yen and concerns \par \par \uc2  about the unsolved kidnappings in the southern Philippines. \par \par \uc2      "We'll take any excuse to buy the dollar. And we know the \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't afford to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with their small \par \par \uc2  reserves," a local bank dealer said. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \par \par \uc2  international reserves stood at $14.44 billion at end-April. \par \par \uc2      But others said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might take the opportunity \par \par \uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  given the market was heavily long of dollars. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  STOCKS REBOUND \par \par \uc2      Hong Kong stocks found late support from a technical \par \par \uc2  rebound after the oversold benchmark index hovered around the \par \par \uc2  13,000 level for most of the day. \par \par \uc2      The Hang Seng Index ended up 1.43 percent at 13,133.66, \par \par \uc2  helped by gains in the two biggest stocks HSBC Holdings and \par \par \uc2  China Mobile. \par \par \uc2      Banking giant HSBC, which had fallen five percent in the \par \par \uc2  past month, rose 1.35 percent to HK$94, while China's biggest \par \par \uc2  mobile phone operator jumped 3.32 percent to HK$40.40 after \par \par \uc2  shedding more than four percent on Monday. \par \par \uc2  China shares also rebounded in late trade with the H-share \par \par \uc2  and the red chip indices mostly recovering their respective two \par \par \uc2  percent deficits to see H-shares end up one percent and the red \par \par \uc2  chips close just 0.30 percent lower. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIA BLAST FELLS RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  News of a bomb blast in Jakarta and another clash between \par \par \uc2  police and students protesting fuel price hikes dragged the \par \par \uc2  rupiah to a two-week low of 11,385 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  Five people were wounded when a powerful bomb exploded at a \par \par \uc2  boarding-house in a Jakarta suburb while police fired teargas \par \par \uc2  to disperse 100 stone-throwing students near a university. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Jakarta said dollar profit-taking by interbank \par \par \uc2  players and some export-related inflows beyond 11,350 capped \par \par \uc2  its downside. They said there was no sign of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  but players were cautious a test of 11,400 may invite \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. $0958 GMT \par \par \uc2  Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     122.83\tab  123.36\tab    +0.43\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8165\tab  1.8166\tab    +0.01\par  Taiwan dlr     34.37\tab   34.38\tab    +0.03\par  Korean won   1300.00\tab 1297.00\tab    -0.23\par  Baht\tab      45.23\tab   45.32\tab    +0.20\par  Peso\tab      52.29\tab   51.98\tab    -0.59\par  Rupiah\tab 11370.00     11280.00\tab    -0.79\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab Latest     End