\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Singapore downplays M&A impact on  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mantik Kusjanto \par \uc2 710 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2001\par 11:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, May 16 (Reuters) - Singapore on Wednesday played down the impact an estimated outflow of up to US$14.5 billion resulting from overseas acquisitions would have on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the amounts are sufficiently large to move the Singapore dollar in a significant way," the Minister of Finance, Richard Hu, said in Parliament on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  But he said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS), would take any short-term impact into account. \par \par \uc2  The finance minister's statement followed recent weakness in the Singapore dollar against the U.S. unit on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, which traders have linked to merger and acquisition flows and market expectations of these flows. \par \par \uc2  Singapore Telecommunications announced in March a planned purchase of Australia's second largest telco, Cable & Wireless Optus Ltd , worth up to US$8.7 billion. \par \par \uc2  When asked about whether there was any change in the terms of the proposed acquisition of Optus, Hu said: "I'm not aware of any significant change." \par \par \uc2  SingTel, currently 78 percent owned by the government, is seeking approval from Australian authorities for its takeover of C&W Optus, which is 52-percent owned by Britain's Cable & Wireless Plc . \par \par \uc2  Separately, DBS Group , in which the government owns 38 percent, has mounted a bid worth up to US$5.8 billion for \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Dao Heng Bank . \par \par \uc2  MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENES\b0 \highlight\uc2  TO SUPPORT SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The MAS, in a rare departure from a policy of no comment, confirmed to a local newspaper, the Business Times, on Wednesday that it has been selling U.S dollars in support of the weak Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "MAS confirmed in writing to BT yesterday that it sold US dollars in the market last week - a departure from its usual practice of not commenting on its actions," the paper reported on its front page on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The newspaper quoted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  saying: "MAS will continue to do so when necessary to offset any market impact of such corporate demand for U.S. dollar against the Sing dollar." \par \par \uc2  The MAS also confirmed that its policy for a modest and gradual appreciation of the Singapore dollar remains unchanged. \par \par \uc2  "This policy is based on our medium term assessment of the economy, not short term economic or inflation developments," the MAS said in its statement. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, which has lost more than four percent since the beginning of the year, edged up to 1.8125/35 0850 GMT, up from fresh 11-year lows hit last week at 1.8265. \par \par \uc2  OVERSEAS ACQUISITIONS OF STRATEGIC INTERESTS \par \par \uc2  Hu said the proposed purchases by Singapore government-linked companies of both Optus and Dao Heng Bank were long term strategic business moves. \par \par \uc2  "Hong Kong is clearly one market, (which) for obvious reasons, is a must for DBS since they are going to be a regional player," Hu said. \par \par \uc2  The government has been encouraging local firms to grow beyond the confines of the Singapore economy for some time. \par \par \uc2  Hu said both acquisitions were such moves, and they would enable them to tap regional opportunities and become more than Singaporean companies. \par \par \uc2  SingTel's expansion plans hit snags twice last year as bids for stakes in Hong Kong's C&W HKT and Malaysia's Time Engineering fell through on perception that the firm was controlled by the Singapore government. \par \par \uc2  In an effort to shake the perception, the eighth largest telecom firm in Asia said last month the Singapore government had given up its special share which offered veto rights over major corporate decisions. \par \par \uc2  Hu said the government did not interfere in the commercial decisions of government-linked firms. \par \par \uc2  "Their soundness (acquisitions) has to be judged over time by the performance of SingTel and DBS, and not by the immediate reaction of the stock market as reflected in their share prices." \par \par \uc2  Hu also defended both acquisitions which were seen too high for the price of growth. \par \par \uc2  "When you buy controlling shares in a large \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , generally you have to pay a substantial premium," he said, adding that the size of the premium was a judgement the management had to take and it was not in the government's interest to challenge. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx5g03qk3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
