\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 2-Asian stocks slide, Nikkei hits 15-yr low. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ben Richardson \par \uc2 1,035 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2001\par 07:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE Feb 28 (Reuters) - Tokyo led most Asian markets lower on Wednesday, with the Nikkei 225 Share Average briefly touching a 15-year low on miserable economic data at home and from the United States. \par \par \uc2  The index fell as far as 12,784.17, its lowest since December 10, 1985, before recovering to 12,883.54 at the close, down 1.35 percent - a 28-month low. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei took its cue from overnight losses on the Nasdaq Composite Index, which fell 4.36 percent on the back of big declines in U.S. consumer confidence and home sales, raising fears the country's economy may be in worse shape than most people think. \par \par \uc2  Adding to the bearish tone were lower than expected figures for Japanese industrial output for January, raising the spectre of recession in the country. \par \par \uc2  Preliminary data released by the Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry before the start of trade showed a shocking 3.9 percent month-on-month drop, much worse than a median forecast of a 0.2 percent rise in a Reuters poll of 13 economists released last week. \par \par \uc2  "It's a very bleak number," said Garry Evans, strategist at HSBC Securities. \par \par \uc2  Fibre-optic shares and high-techs - such as Nikon Corp - played a large part in the fall. \par \par \uc2  Furukawa Electric Co Ltd, Japan's top optical fibre maker, came under pressure after shares of JDS Uniphase Corp, the world's top supplier of fibre optic components, fell 15 percent in New York. \par \par \uc2  EURO MISSES OPPORTUNITY \par \par \uc2  On the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the euro failed to make much of the dire figures from the United States and Japan, holding around 106.65 yen and easing back to $0.9180 after an early bounce failed to clear resistance at $0.9185. \par \par \uc2  The yen proved remarkably resilient, thanks in large part to exporters' hedging forward receipts and the fact that Japanese markets have long discounted a return to recession and almost given up hope of a policy remedy. \par \par \uc2  "We can't get any gloomier," one dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 116.18 yen compared with 116.20 before the Japanese data. \par \par \uc2  Markets are now focused on the BOJ policy meeting taking place on Wednesday and Federal Reserve Board chairman Alan Greenspan's speech later in the day. \par \par \uc2  Investors are looking for signs of a more aggressive stance from both to boost the economies of their respective countries. \par \par \uc2  News that Greenspan would take the unusual step of altering the monetary policy testimony he delivered two weeks ago to a Senate panel as he goes before the House Finance Committee has fanned speculation in markets eager for signs the Fed will cut rates soon. \par \par \uc2  But few analysts see either \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  responding to the soft data immediately. \par \par \uc2  HSBC DRAGS \par \par \uc2  In Hong Kong, the benchmark Hang Seng Index was dragged lower by global banking group HSBC Holdings, which fell 3.26 percent, extending Tuesday's losses after poor earnings. \par \par \uc2  The index was down 1.66 percent to 14,589.17 at midday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said investors sold HSBC shares on lingering concerns that a slowing U.S. economy would hurt the bank's earnings. \par \par \uc2  The H-share index of mainland companies listed in Hong Kong fell 4.53 percent, contrary to market expectations. \par \par \uc2  Investors had been expecting the index to jump in tandem with gains in B shares following the resumption of trade in the Shanghai and Shenzhen markets on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Investors shifted their funds to China's offshore oil exploration company, CNOOC Ltd, which made its Hong Kong debut on Wednesday, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Shares of CNOOC Ltd, China's third largest petroleum company, closed midday at HK$6.70, up 12.6 percent from an issue price of HK$5.95 per share before brokerage fees. \par \par \uc2  CHINA B MARKET SOARS \par \par \uc2  The much-heralded reopening of China's B share market panned out as predicted by analysts with both the Shenzhen and Shanghai indices soaring near their 10 percent daily limit on minute volume. \par \par \uc2  It was the first trading day since February 19, when China allowed citizens with legal \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounts to buy B shares and opened the door for a potential $75 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits to be invested. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect a sustained rally of 30-50 percent as investors plug the valuation gap between B shares and local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  A shares. \par \par \uc2  There were few sellers when trading resumed, however, and new punters could only watch with envy as existing B share investors saw nine years of patience finally pay off. \par \par \uc2  "If you already hold B shares, you are very lucky, but if you play the game now it's probably a fool's game," said James Cheng, of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund house Asia Strategic Investment Management Ltd. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  PROP \par \par \uc2  Korean stocks held onto marginal gains, as heavy buying by investment trust management companies that had received funds from the National Pension Fund helped offset the gloomy news from abroad. \par \par \uc2  The National Pension Fund said last Friday it would invest 160 billion won ($128.2 million) this week in the stock market, and an undetermined amount in March. \par \par \uc2  An official from the pension fund said it put 160 billion won into three investment trust companies, Samsung, LG and Hyundai Investment Trust Management. \par \par \uc2  The presidential Blue House said earlier this month the nation's pension funds would expand their stock investments to 25 trillion won from eight trillion won. \par \par \uc2  The Korea Composite Stock Price Index was up 0.09 percent to 578.10 points at the close. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan market was closed for Peace Memorial Day. \par \par \uc2  Oil prices on Wednesday were lifted from seven-week lows after weekly data from the United States showed only a small recovery in lean U.S. crude inventories. \par \par \uc2  U.S. light crude in electronic trade edged up 12 cents to $28.25 a barrel after industry group the American Petroleum Institute (API) said crude stocks rose a modest 2.2 million barrels in the week to February 23. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday's uptick interrupted a steady slide in oil prices that has seen U.S. crude fall $4.50 a barrel over the past five weeks. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx2s05esl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
