\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-HKMA says HK banking system robust, profits rise. \b0\par\par\uc2 369 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2001\par 09:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Jan 11 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) said on Thursday that the territory's banking sector remained robust as the economy and operating conditions improved. \par \par \uc2  The territory's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said the total assets of Hong Kong's Exchange Fund, which backs the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was HK$1,020.8 billion at the end of 2000, up 1.8 percent from 1999. \par \par \uc2  In a presentation to legislators, the HKMA said that the pre-tax operating profits of local banks had grown 45.7 percent year-on-year in the first nine months of 2000 which represented a sharp rise compared with 15.2 percent growth in 1999. \par \par \uc2  "Strong growth was mainly attributed to a significant drop in bad debt charges and increases in net interest and fee income," the HKMA said. \par \par \uc2  Banks' asset quality had continued to improve slowly, with charges for bad debts falling 54.8 percent in the first three quarters of last year, it said. \par \par \uc2  The asset quality of banks' mortgage portfolio remained relatively low though the delinquency ratio rose to 1.31 percent in November versus 1.13 percent at the end of 1999. \par \par \uc2  Banks remained well capitalised and highly liquid, as reflected in their high capital adequacy ratio, which came in at 18.7 percent, and their liquidity ratio of 50.2 percent as of September 2000. \par \par \uc2  The territory's Exchange Fund recorded modest profit for 2000 though it shed HK$11.6 billion on its equity portfolio because of falls on the Hang Seng , it said. \par \par \uc2  The government still had HK$150.7 billion (US$19.3 billion) worth of Hong Kong stocks at the end of 2000, including stocks transferred from the Land Fund in 1998. \par \par \uc2  The contents of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's equity portfolio was largely amassed when the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market to fend off speculators in 1998. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA said it intended to keep only around HK$50 billion worth of its remaining equity portfolio in accordance with investment guidelines laid out by the Exchange Fund. It did not say when it intended to reach this level. \par \par \uc2  (US$ = Hk$7.8). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx1b01y18\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
