\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Mixed, outlook bleak on U.S. worries. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,437 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2001\par 10:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 9 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were a mixed bag late Tuesday with some consolidating around recent weak levels, but overall the mood was depressed on worries about the impact a cooling U.S. economy could have on the region. \par \par \uc2  "Global investors are still very much focused on the majors and there is very little risk appetite for emerging markets like Asia," said Andrew Fung, head of Asia Pacific research at Rabobank. \par \par \uc2  Fung said weakness of the yen on top of the U.S.-led global slowdown would hit exports and growth throughout the region. \par \par \uc2  Morgan Stanley Dean Witter in a research report on Tuesday said Asian governments were likely to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken to cushion against an export downturn as the U.S. economy went into recession in the first half. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. investment bank warned on Monday that the U.S. economy would probably sink into recession in the first half of 2001, breaking its longest expansion in history. \par \par \uc2  It said the won and Taiwan dollar looked the most vulnerable, facing declines of about 10 percent before bouncing back with the U.S. recovery late in the year. \par \par \uc2  It said the peso, baht and rupiah would respond more to domestic politics rather than to changes in the global demand scenario, while the Singapore dollar was likely to follow the North Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The yen was steady around 115.90, off last week's 17-month lows against the dollar while profit-taking softened the euro from last week's six-month peaks. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht traded in a tight range of 42.79-43.00, caught between foreign investors looking to sell dollars and local corporate demand for dollars. \par \par \uc2  Though it briefly slipped to 43.00, dealers in Bangkok said the post-election mood was still upbeat. "We're still optimistic about a stable outcome from the elections," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  "The baht might be consolidating around these levels. We're now waiting to see who (Thai Rak Thai Party leader) Thaksin (Shinawatra) will choose as Finance Minister," he added. \par \par \uc2  Telecoms tycoon Thaksin won the weekend general election by a landslide, according to unofficial results. \par \par \uc2  However, the victory has not entirely dispelled political uncertainty in Thailand as Thaksin is awaiting a court decision that determines his immediate fate. \par \par \uc2  A constitutional court is expected to rule in the coming months on whether Thaksin was guilty of hiding assets. If found guilty, he would be barred from taking office for five years. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah mustered some strength to rise above the 9,500 level on the back of dollar selling from foreign banks in Jakarta earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Jakarta said the dollar offers were spurred by the stronger baht and Singapore dollar, but met with dollar bids from local banks buying on behalf of corporates. \par \par \uc2  State banks were also spotted selling dollars, though only in small amounts. A dealer at a U.S. bank in Jakarta said volume was around $200 million by late afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The market was also watching Bank Indonesia's SBI note auction on Wednesday, and some dealers said they believed the bank may hold on rates after last week's rate cuts by the Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's chief economics minister Rizal Ramli warned on Monday against excessively high interest rates. Rates have been steadily creeping higher in recent months to shore up support for the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia has said it would decide this week on whether to follow the U.S. rate cut. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso made slow progress above the 51 per dollar mark, hovering in a tight nine-tick band on light volume of $77 million, below Monday's already thin $93 million. \par \par \uc2  A lack of commercial demand and wariness over the impeachment saga dampened activity, though Manila stocks did manage a solid 2.5 percent rise, with brokers even citing foreign buying of selected blue chips. \par \par \uc2  They said offshore investors were beginning to position for a decision on President Joseph Estrada's impeachment trial, slated to end around February 12. The prospect of lower interest rates also helped. \par \par \uc2  Estrada said on Tuesday economic and political stability would be restored once he was acquitted of corruption charges. \par \par \uc2  But in a reminder of the problems besetting the economy, Finance Secretary Jose Pardo said the budget deficit for 2000 was likely to be below 140 billion pesos, above an official estimate of 130 billion pesos made only last week. \par \par \uc2  The deficit for the first 11 months of 2000 was 114.38 billion pesos, dwarfing the government's original 62 billion peso target. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar climbed on the back of rallying local equities, with investors hoping the beleaguered stock market was making a long-awaited rebound. \par \par \uc2  But dealers in Hong Kong noted that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the market around the Taiwan dollar's intraday high of 32.695 to buy U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  "They were there chiefly to smooth the Taiwan dollar's appreciation," said a chief dealer at a European bank in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the local dollar held onto most of its gains as local and foreign banks continued to unwind long dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  While dealers said there were still some of these positions that would likely be liquidated this week, they also noted that the Taiwan dollar had appreciated against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its regional competitors. \par \par \uc2  The cross rate between the Taiwan dollar and yen stood at 3.5461 at 0819 GMT, which some dealers and analysts said may be too high for the authorities' liking. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, Taiwan's economy is expected to show signs of slowing, as it is particularly exposed to the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  December trade data released on Monday showed import growth slowing to a one percent rise on year, largely due to a seven percent contraction on year in capital equipment imports. Analysts said this was a worrying reflection of sluggish domestic demand, and with exports growth expected to slow, Taiwan could face tougher times. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The Korean won firmed from early levels helped by foreign portfolio inflows, which prompted local exporters to offload their holdings. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said most participants in the market were reluctant to unwind dollar longs given a depressing export and growth outlook amid a slowing U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors were net buyers of about 35 billion won worth of local shares, adding to shares worth 144 billion won they bought on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a steadier yen also helped sentiment but offshore players were active in the forward market to hedge their equity investments. \par \par \uc2  The six-month non-deliverable forward dollar/won was at 1,255.9/57.3 from 1,260.6/62.1 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar hovered around the 1.7300 mark drawing some support from a steadier baht which kept most players cautious on going long U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  A steadier won and the Taiwan dollar also helped sentiment in the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The volatility in the last few days has made local corporates a bit cautious but broadly speaking everyone in the market is looking at weaker levels for the Singapore dollar," a dealer at a U.S. bank said. \par \par \uc2  He said Singapore dollar's failure to convincingly break 1.7280, where local corporates were spotted buying U.S. dollars, would encourage bears. \par \par \uc2  He said the Singapore dollar was likely to move into a lower range of 1.7330-80 in the coming days as the post-election euphoria in the baht market settled down. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0923 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     115.81\tab  116.27\tab    +0.40\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.7323\tab  1.7295\tab    -0.16\par  Taiwan dlr    32.695\tab  32.789\tab    +0.29\par  Korean won   1254.50\tab 1265.50\tab    +0.88\par  Baht\tab      43.06\tab   43.03\tab    -0.07\par  Peso\tab      50.85\tab   50.91\tab    +0.12\par  Rupiah\tab  9500.00\tab 9505.00\tab    +0.05\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     115.81\tab  114.39\tab    -1.23\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.7323\tab  1.7365\tab    +0.24\par  Taiwan dlr    32.695\tab  32.900\tab    +0.63\par  Korean won   1254.50\tab 1277.00\tab    +1.79\par  Baht\tab      43.06\tab   43.70\tab    +1.49\par  Peso\tab      50.85\tab   50.75\tab    -0.20\par  Rupiah\tab  9500.00\tab 9470.00\tab    -0.32\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Doku