\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Plagued by economic, political demons. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,498 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Januar 2001\par 09:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 3 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dipped late Wednesday in otherwise lacklustre trading with economic and political woes lurking in the background. \par \par \uc2  Regional \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets took their cue from flagging U.S. markets as further evidence emerged of a more serious than expected slowdown in the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. National Association of Purchasing Management's index fell to 43.7 in December - its lowest level since April 1991, and well below forecasts of a 47.0 reading. \par \par \uc2  While Asia is vulnerable to a sharper than anticipated downturn in the U.S. economy, analysts have also said a global economic slowdown will shift the stress on regional developments. \par \par \uc2  And with labour unrest in Korea, a nascent banking crisis in Taiwan, and political uncertainty in the Philippines, Indonesia, and Thailand, Asia has not been looking its best lately. \par \par \uc2  "On the one hand, you have the weaker dollar. But on the other hand, the regional picture doesn't look so good either," said a chief dealer at a European bank in Singapore. "It's the final nail in the coffin - a weaker U.S. economy." \par \par \uc2  The region's largest economy, Japan, has been showing signs of distress as well, causing the yen to slip against the dollar and euro . The euro moved higher on growing optimism about Europe's economy relative to Japan and the U.S. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won recovered after weakening sharply to 1,292 in early trading, with local banks selling dollars in a thin market. \par \par \uc2  Korea Development Bank and other state banks were among the sellers during the session, sparking speculation the authorities were behind the selling. \par \par \uc2  Two senior officials at the Finance Ministry expressed displeasure over the won's weakness, with one calling the recent dollar-buying "irrational" and the other threatening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a European bank in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said a major U.S. investment bank sold U.S. dollars in options-related trade. He said the bank had been steadily selling U.S. dollars since late Tuesday, and had sold a relatively large amount around 1,290. \par \par \uc2  The won's weak start in the new year has largely been attributed to a waning local stock market, which has been roiled by a pessimistic economic outlook, and importer settlements. \par \par \uc2  Korea's financial markets have also been hurt by the slow progress of corporate reforms and recent labour unrest. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar kept to the wrong side of 33 per dollar but fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limited the downside. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said declines in the won and yen and worries about a slowing U.S. economy and global tech-sector demand weighed on sentiment. \par \par \uc2  UBS Warburg suggested players take long U.S. dollar/Taiwan dollar positions, arguing that falling exports and an ailing financial sector would weigh on the island's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It said in a research note Taiwan's economy was in trouble from both external and domestic factors and advised clients to go long the dollar at 32.90 with a target of 35 and stops at 31.95. \par \par \uc2  UBS Warburg said last week's interest rate cuts suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had decided "to engineer a controlled devaluation of Taiwan dollar as a response to worsening fundamentals". \par \par \uc2  It said a break above 33.194, the November 30 low, was expected to take the Taiwan dollar to the next key support of 34.851, last seen in August 1998. A breach of this level could pull the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  towards a long-term objective of 40. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was supported by dollar selling by some local banks related to what dealers said was local corporate investment flows. Low volumes exaggerated the move. \par \par \uc2  They said the Singapore dollar rose to a day high of 1.7305 from late Tuesday's 1.7351 as the inflows prompted other players to trim their long dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  But strong dollar demand near the lows eroded the gains. \par \par \uc2  "There were some investment flows and the market is still quite thin so the dollar/Sing eased," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  The rising euro, at around five-month highs against the U.S. dollar, also helped sentiment. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to be a significant component of the trade-weighted basket Singapore uses to manage its exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said weakness of other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the yen, also believed to have a sizeable share of the basket, were likely to cap its upside. \par \par \uc2  "The net weakness of the U.S. dollar on a trade weighted basis is likely to help but direction of other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have its bearing," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah found its new year's rally short-lived, with dealers in Jakarta blaming local bank dollar purchases on behalf of corporates for Wednesday's decline. \par \par \uc2  It was still well off recent lows of around 9,700, which were hit in holiday-thinned offshore trade, but back the wrong side of 9,500 with foreign banks also on the bid for dollars. \par \par \uc2  Data released on Wednesday shows deteriorating economic picture with trade surplus shrinking to $1.52 billion in November from $2.03 billion in October as exports fall 7.5 percent and imports rise 2.9 percent. \par \par \uc2  Inflation rose to 9.35 percent in December compared with 9.12 percent the month before. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht pulled away from its overnight lows, helped by dollar profit-taking by local and foreign banks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Bangkok said the dollar had been creeping into overbought territory in a market already hugely long on dollars, but was now edging away from the overbought mark of 70 on the 14-day relative strength index, to stand at 66.2. \par \par \uc2  But the baht's gains were seen capped around 43.40/50, which is around the 62 percent retracement of the dollar's November 21-December 22 decline. \par \par \uc2  Dealers added that political uncertainty ahead of the January 6 poll still hung over the market. Front-runner Thaksin Sinawatra was indicted last week on corruption charges, and faces a five-year ban from politics if found guilty. \par \par \uc2  But the court proceedings could take months, possibly leaving the country in limbo if Thaksin's party wins the elections. \par \par \uc2  On the offshore baht forward market, the one-month forward was at -1/3 satang per dollar compared with -2.5/1.6 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso hovered around the 51 per dollar mark posting a day high of 50.65 and a low of 51.17 as players remained wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should go back soon to 49-50 range. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Manila said most of the activity was interbank as other players and corporates stayed out amid uncertainty surrounding President Joseph Estrada's impeachment trial. \par \par \uc2  Rafael Buenaventura said volatility in the peso was "very temporary" and the exchange rate should settle in the 49-50 range where it was before the spate of bombings over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  He also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Buenaventura earlier said the government wanted to cut key overnight interest rates but deeper cuts were not possible because of a large fiscal deficit. \par \par \uc2  He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trying to bring overnight rates to around 11-12 percent. The overnight borrowing rate now stands at 13.5 percent and the lending rate at 15.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine budget deficit had overshot the projected level of 62.5 billion pesos and soared to 114.438 billion pesos as of November last year. Recent official estimates place it at around 120 billion pesos. \par \par \uc2  The country's Senate will hold a special session starting next week to discuss the 2001 national budget. The session has been delayed because of Estrada's impeachment trial. \par \par \uc2  After Senate approval, a bicameral panel would be formed to draft a final version for clearance by both houses of Congress. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0840 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     114.43\tab  114.67\tab    +0.21\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.7317\tab  1.7351\tab    +0.20\par  Taiwan dlr    33.033\tab  32.943\tab    -0.27\par  Korean won   1270.00\tab 1275.00\tab    +0.39\par  Baht\tab      43.58\tab   43.55\tab    -0.07\par  Peso\tab      51.00\tab   50.80\tab    -0.39\par  Rupiah\tab  9520