\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Steady, but pressure building. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,857 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Dezember 2000\par 02:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 14 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were steady early Thursday, but may come attack later in the day as concerns over U.S. economic growth overshadowed the presidential election drama. \par \par \uc2  While U.S. blue chips gained some ground after the Supreme Court ruled in favour of Republican George W. Bush, the Nasdaq was hit by worries over slowing growth in the technology sector. \par \par \uc2  The world's No. 1 personal computer maker, Compaq Computer Corp. , fell 13 percent after it issued a profit warning late Tuesday. Regional technology-driven bourses in Taiwan and Korea stand to lose the most from the Nasdaq's 3.72 percent fall. \par \par \uc2  However, investors are hoping that a cut in U.S. interest rates early next year might bring some cheer. U.S. retail sales data issued Wednesday showed a 0.4 percent drop in November after October's flat growth, adding to increasing evidence that the U.S. economy is slowing down. \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood firm against the euro and yen , which is suffering from concerns over the Japanese economy. A major Japanese newspaper Asahi Shimbun said in a report on Thursday the Economic Planning Agency would revise July-September growth to below zero from 0.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  Markets in Asia are also facing a raft of domestic woes on top of a depressing external environment. Investors in the Philippines have been largely sidelined during the impeachment trial of President Joseph Estrada. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia, which is coping with separatist movements as well as snail-paced economic reforms, has yet to ratify changes to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, as the bank's governor awaits trial. The major debt restructuring case of Thai Petrochemical Industry and upcoming elections are weighing on Thai markets. \par \par \uc2  North Asia is suffering from its own headaches over economic growth and corporate and financial reform. Korea's largest commercial bank Kookmin Bank said early Thursday it had suspended its merger talks with Housing and Commercial Bank due to protests from labour unions. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar was likely to come under renewed pressure as offshore players seek to test the local dollar's support despite expected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  have said the general market sentiment is that the Taiwan dollar is overvalued given the weaker yen and won, as well as the numerous domestic political and economic issues weighing on Taiwan. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers in Singapore said both domestic and foreign players increasingly believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had no choice but to give up its dogged defence of the Taiwan dollar as it is providing the only support to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The bank was believed to have spent $300 million to $400 million on Wednesday in preventing the local dollar from falling past 33.10. \par \par \uc2  With more profit warnings from U.S. tech companies and falls on the Nasdaq and Philadelphia Semiconductor Index , Taiwan equities are expected to fall, adding more pressure to the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On the non-deliverable forward market, the six-month Taiwan dollar was at 0.76/84 and the one-year was at 0.91/96. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht moved in a narrow range, well supported around 43.65-43.70 as baht liquidity offshore tightened. \par \par \uc2  The one-month offshore forward rate edged up to 5.5-10.5 satang per dollar while the onshore rate of the same tenor was steady at -5.0/-0.3 satang. \par \par \uc2  "There's still good demand for dollars out there. But the direction of the baht would perhaps hinge largely on the liquidity," said a dealer at a U.S. bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it hoped Thai banks would lend more to the business sector to stimulate economic growth next year. \par \par \uc2  However, some analysts doubt if that would happen, given the amount of bad assets carried by the sector and threats of banks having to do the second or third round of recapitalisation as the economy slows and asset quality deteriorates. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso was little moved in early offshore trade as uncertainty over the impeachment trial of President Joseph Estrada and ahead of Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy meeting kept players cautious. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the peso to move in a weak but narrow range with inflows from overseas workers limiting losses and demand for dollars from local corporates capping the upside. \par \par \uc2  An expected slowing of remittances from overseas workers in the second half of the month was likely to pull the peso down towards 50-50.50 to the dollar in the coming days, a dealer at a European bank in Manila said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has hinted at a cut of 50-100 basis points in its key overnight borrowing and lending rates. \par \par \uc2  The rates have been cut twice in the past two weeks by a total of 100 basis points, after they were jacked up in October by four percentage points. \par \par \uc2  The overnight borrowing rate now stands at 14 percent and the lending rate at 16.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the impeachment trial and the uncertainty over its decision on the fate of Estrada could drag on well into the next year's first quarter. \par \par \uc2  But whatever the decision, the economy is expected to slow, further worsening its fiscal problems and keeping sentiment on the peso depressed, they said. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund echoed these concerns on Wednesday, saying the government urgently needed to rein in its budget deficit, which it said was likely to come in this year at double the original target. \par \par \uc2  It said the Philippine economy should grow by 4.0 percent this year but that growth would slow in 2001 because of political worries and a host of external and domestic economic problems. \par \par \uc2  The statement, also expressing worries over a swelling budget deficit, slow pace of structural reforms and foreign investment inflows, was issued at the end of the annual policy review of the Philippine economy. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won gapped lower at the open mainly due to pressure from falls in local equities as well as persistent poor local sentiment over the country's economic future. \par \par \uc2  Renewed concerns over profits at U.S. technology companies forced the Nasdaq lower, also hitting the benchmark Korean stock index as local tech heavyweights lose ground. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was generally more bullish on the dollar following the Supreme Court ruling that will likely result in Republican George W. Bush becoming the U.S. president. \par \par \uc2  They added that the market was very thin, and therefore, more subject to volatility. \par \par \uc2  Korea is also facing other domestic woes outside of the technology sector, whose weakness has brought forecasts of slowing exports and economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The government-led corporate and financial restructuring programme received a blow when Korea's largest commercial bank Kookmin Bank announced it had suspended merger talks with Housing and Commercial Bank due to increasing protests from labour unions. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Korea is expected to announce measures to ease an ongoing corporate cash crunch later in the day. Dealers said they did not expect the measures to influence the spot market. \par \par \uc2  On the NDF market, offshore players have been buying U.S. dollars in the three-month and six-month tenors, which were both quoted at 1,194/95. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar was hovering the wrong side of 1.74 amid talk the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore was in the market again overnight to buy the U.S. dollar from 1.7370, pushing it to highs of around 1.7420. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was good two-way interest for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early trade, but fears the MAS might be trying to prevent an appreciation of the Sing dollar against its trade-weighted basket might cap the upside. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has hovered near or in overbought territory last week and early this week. It is now trading in the neutral zone on the 14-day relative strength index, with a reading of 46.186. \par \par \uc2  "Given fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the dollar's strength against the yen and the euro, the dollar should be well-supported against the Sing at 1.7400," said a dealer at a U.S. bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah traded with a steadier tone in light volumes amid wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that dealers said pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up from an intraday low of around 9,550 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a European bank in Singapore said offshore players were already staying away from the volatile rupiah but dollar demand from importers and foreign debt repayment needs of local private firms were likely to keep it under pressure. \par \par \uc2  "It is onshore demand that is pushing dollar/rupiah up. It's mostly local banks who will come to buy dollars for their clients and then come to sell dollars for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said several players were caught long dollars after Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and were likely to cover their positions which could give the rupiah a firm tone. \par \par \uc2  "But eventually the dollar demand in the market will pull it down," the dealer added. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's weakness comes despite a new government rule that state firms can only buy dollars from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It is estimated that state firms' average monthly dollar purchases are between $500 million to $750 million - around a third of the market's total dollar demand. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said outstanding private foreign debt which stood at the end of the third quarter at $68.2 billion, against Indonesia's total external debt of $143.3 billion, was enough to keep the rupiah depressed. \par \par \uc2  Private foreign debt repayment needs in December alone are estimated in a range of $3-$7 billion. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market will closely watch the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board of governors' presentation to parliament on controversial amendments to the bank's laws expected next week. \par \par \uc2  A special parliamentary committee will sit through a month-long recess to deal with the amendments. Parliament resumes sitting in full around mid-January when a decision on the amendments is expected. \par \par \uc2  If passed, the amended laws would give parliament the power to dismiss any of the board's eight members who fail to meet certain performance standards. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0131 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight