\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 2-Asia shares mixed - Japan, HK down on Nasdaq. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ramthan Hussain \par \uc2 708 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2000\par 09:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 7 (Reuters) - Asian share markets were mixed on Thursday with Japan and Hong Kong hit by Wall Street's fall on a gloomy earnings outlook from Apple Computer but Korea and Taiwan making some headway in late trade. \par \par \uc2  European stock markets were also expected to weaken after the sharp reversal of sentiment on Wall Street, traders said. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, high tech stocks such as Softbank Corp and Sony Corp foundered on a setback in their Nasdaq-listed counterparts, dragging indices lower despite a firm "old economy" performance. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei 225 average finished 1.14 percent lower at 14,720.36 while the broader TOPIX index slipped 0.93 percent at 1,368.17. \par \par \uc2  "It looks like cheaper, non-technology stocks are lending quite a bit of support to the market," said Toshihiro Koizumi, fund manager at Chuo Mitsui Asset Management. \par \par \uc2  "We still think technology shares will recover into the new year, but there will likely be a bit of volatility and defensives and old economy stocks will likely lend support along the way." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. market sank on Wednesday after Apple warned that soft holiday sales would put it in the red for the quarter. \par \par \uc2  The Nasdaq Composite Index fell 3.23 percent at 2,796.50 after scoring its biggest one-day rise ever the previous day when it jumped over 10 percent on speculation the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might soon cut interest rates. \par \par \uc2  In after-hours trading the Apple news continued to bite with shares of computer giant International Business Machines Corp extending losses. \par \par \uc2  EURO HIGHEST SINCE SEPT 22 \par \par \uc2  The news also hit regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealings, taking profit warnings from U.S. companies back to the fore of the market's focus for the Asian units. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was firmer against the yen in late afternoon Tokyo trade, but was off its intraday high of 110.60 yen on profit-taking and caution ahead of U.S. jobs data. The market was focusing on U.S. November nonfarm payrolls data to be released on Friday, for signs of scope of slowdown in U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  The euro rose to $0.8953, the highest level since $0.9040 hit on Sept 22, when the Group of Seven (G7) industrialised countries jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  SEOUL, TAIWAN, AUSTRALIA RECOVERS \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  stocks fell on Wall Street weakness and investor disappointment over prices paid for two development sites in a govenrment land auction. The Hang Seng Index lost 1.24 percent to 14,911.22 in afternoon trade. \par \par \uc2  Seoul tech shares were hit hard in early trading, dragging the Korea Composite Stock Price Index (KOSPI) down 0.69 percent. But the KOSPI rebounded in late trade to gain 1.14 percent to 523.78, spurred by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to keep call rates on hold in December. \par \par \uc2  The rebound buoyed hopes for a year-end rally as lower interest rates may switch money back into underperforming equities, fund managers said. But the upside was capped by overnight losses in U.S. chip and computer stocks. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan stocks nudged higher on bargain hunting following early losses triggered by Nasdaq's pullback and Standard and Poor's downgrade of Taiwan's economic outlook. The benchmark index ended up 0.81 percent at 5,212.73. \par \par \uc2  Australian shares ended mixed with resources issues taking heart from stronger commodity prices, while earnings concerns emerged elsewhere. The S&P/ASX 200 index ended just 0.9 of a point higher at 3,309.0. \par \par \uc2  Singapore shares were lower in afternoon trade on selling in stocks that could be affected if Morgan Stanley Capital International (MSCI) adopts a free-float system for its widely tracked indices. The Straits Times Index was down 1.16 percent to 1,952.00 by 0712 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Oil prices crept back above $30 per barrel as a blast of Arctic air chilled much of the United States and sent U.S. natural gas and home heating fuel prices soaring. \par \par \uc2  U.S. light crude futures stood at $30.26 a barrel at 0627 GMT, a rise of 41 cents from the New York settlement of $29.85. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwc70aj3i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
