\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 :  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Blocks Taiwan Dlr Fall \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2000\par 09:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- Massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  blocked a potential free-fall in the New Taiwan dollar during Asian dealing Monday as market participants scrambled to sell the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  following opposition candidate Chen Shui-bian's victory in Saturday's presidential election. \par \par \uc2  Trading in other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was relatively quiet, with most finishing local hours a touch weaker against the U.S. dollar as markets priced in a rise in U.S. interest rates Tuesday, when the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee meets. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Taipei described across the board demand to buy U.S. dollars as traders, investors and commercial customers sought to cover their exposure to the Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , afraid of heightened tensions with mainland China and domestic political instability following the weekend's poll. \par \par \uc2  At US$1.364 billion, trading volumes in the onshore spot market amounted to over twice the normal daily volume. Despite the pressure, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China - the Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - held firm, selling enough U.S. dollars to cap the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its intraday high of NT$30.799, and driving the U.S. dollar down again before the close. \par \par \uc2  Drawing confidence from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's determination to stamp out market volatility and prevent the U.S. dollar breaking up through NT$30.800, most participants believed the storm would soon blow over. \par \par \uc2  "This has all happened before," said the treasurer of one major foreign bank in Taipei. "It's the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy not to let the market panic, and I don't think we'll see any deviation from that any time soon." \par \par \uc2  At the close of local trading, the U.S. dollar was quoted at NT$30.791, up from NT$30.766 at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  In the offshore market, rates on short-dated U.S. dollar/New Taiwan dollar non-deliverable forwards briefly spiked to a premium from a discount. \par \par \uc2  Toward the end of Asian trading, however, NDF rates had eased back to a discount, indicating that the market attaches no risk premium to holding New Taiwan dollars, and that players still anticipate local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation over the longer term. \par \par \uc2  "Watch the equity market," advised Rebecca Patterson, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan in Singapore, explaining that moves in the New Taiwan dollar correlate closely with foreign portfolio flows into Taiwanese stocks. \par \par \uc2  Although Taiwan's benchmark stock index fell by 2.6% Monday despite support from government funds, foreign institutions were net buyers of local equities, according to the stock exchange. \par \par \uc2  "It's our view that the pressure on Taiwanese equities and the New Taiwan dollar will fade fairly quickly," Patterson said, citing Taiwan's positive economic outlook and the conciliatory stance President-elect Chen has so far adopted toward Beijing. \par \par \uc2  In other regional markets, the South Korean won closed moderately lower following U.S. dollar purchases executed by the state-run Korea Development Bank. At the Seoul close, the dollar was at 1,118.00 won, compared with KRW1,117.30 Friday. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia, the Singapore dollar slipped a touch in line with the local stock market as dealers pointed to market jitters over Taiwan and expectations of higher U.S. interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7189, up from S$1.7125 Friday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Indonesian rupiah the dollar was at 7,453 rupiah, up marginally from IDR7,441 late in Asia Friday. \par \par \uc2  Against the Thai baht, the dollar nudged a fraction higher to THB38.005, from THB37.995 Friday, despite strong fourth quarter gross domestic product data released Monday. \par \par \uc2  On the Philippine Dealing System, the peso ended the session little changed, with the dollar at 40.960 pesos, compared with PHP40.963 at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  SUBSCRIBERS CAN FIND ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  COMMENT ON: \par \par \uc2  TELERATE PAGES [1136] OR [4250] \par \par \uc2  DOW JONES NEWSWIRES BY SEARCHING THE CODE N/FXW \par \par \uc2  BLOOMBERG BY ENTERING NI FRX \par \par \uc2  REUTERS BY ENTERING CODE \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /DJ \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw3k01iys\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
