\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Range-Bound; Govt Talks Won Down \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 680 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2000\par 09:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- A barrage of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  backed by dollar purchases executed by state-run banks took some of the recent shine off the South Korean won Tuesday, forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower on the day. \par \par \uc2  Trading activity elsewhere in the region was muted, with most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  edging slightly higher in tightly range-bound markets. \par \par \uc2  Repeated comments from the South Korean finance ministry triggered a wave of dollar short-covering against the won Tuesday, as the Seoul authorities stressed they don't want to see a sharp rise in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Officials have been engaged in a firefighting exercise to counter won appreciation since last Friday, when South Korea's newly appointed Minister of Finance and Economy Lee Hun-jai indicated that the government would in future be less inclined to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to suppress the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Lee's remarks, reported in the Korean press, sparked heavy won buying by local and international market participants which on Monday carried the won to its highest level since November 1997. \par \par \uc2  The rally prompted finance ministry officials to repeat warnings Tuesday morning that the government remains prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to damp excessive market volatility. \par \par \uc2  Those threats were lent further weight later Tuesday by reports that Lee had said there was no sound reason for the won's abrupt appreciation. \par \par \uc2  "The rise in the won due to non-financial factors could spur wrong expectations and that is why the government needs to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Lee was quoted as saying by Korea's Yonhap News Agency. \par \par \uc2  Lee's efforts to talk down the won, backed by dollar-buying from the state-run Korea Development Bank, proved effective Tuesday as market players moved to cover their short positions in the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  By the end of local trading the dollar had been driven up to KRW1,127.00, from KRW1,121.00 at Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in North Asia, the New Taiwan dollar ended the day flat, as U.S. dollar purchases executed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offset local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying by foreign investors. \par \par \uc2  At the Taipei close, the U.S. dollar was at NT$30.837, compared with NT$30.835 the day before. \par \par \uc2  Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended local trading hours mostly a touch higher, despite an early selloff in the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment toward the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to suffer from the weekend's speculation that cabinet minister General Wiranto may be given his marching orders, potentially precipitating a military coup. \par \par \uc2  The market's mood was further clouded by news that religious unrest had spread to the island of Lombok and reports of demonstrations by dispossessed farmers on the island of Bintan, which neighbors Singapore. \par \par \uc2  In response the dollar was bid up to touch IDR7,300, only to ease back again after encountering firm offers at that level. \par \par \uc2  With dollar-buying interest seen at levels between IDR7,150 and IDR7,200, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to range tightly against the rupiah over the short term. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia Tuesday, the dollar was quoted at IDR7,262, down slightly from IDR7,278 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  With little news to guide the market, trading in the Singapore dollar and Thai baht was led by activity in the rupiah Tuesday. After being bid up to a high of S$1.6750 against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the U.S. dollar slipped to end Asian hours at S$1.6714, down from S$1.6742 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Against the baht, the dollar eased to THB37.365, from THB37.425 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  In Manila, the Philippine peso bucked the regional trend, ending lower in relatively light trading. \par \par \uc2  At the close, the dollar was at PHP40.605, compared with PHP40.564 the day before. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  SUBSCRIBERS CAN FIND ASIAN \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  COMMENT ON: \par \par \uc2  TELERATE PAGES [1136] OR [4250] \par \par \uc2  DOW JONES NEWSWIRES BY SEARCHING THE CODE N/FXW \par \par \uc2  BLOOMBERG BY ENTERING NI FRX \par \par \uc2  REUTERS BY ENTERING CODE \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /DJ \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw1i00fhc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
