\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Lose Ground As Govt Forces Won Down \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Dezember 1999\par 09:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  coupled with threats of further official measures to hold down the won knocked the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  off Wednesday's two-year high during Asian trading hours Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Asia's other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also lost ground, but for the most part, activity elsewhere was subdued as markets wind down ahead of the year's end. \par \par \uc2  In a speech delivered Thursday, South Korean Minister of Finance and Economy Kang Bong-kyun said he was "deeply concerned" by the won's recent surge against the U.S. dollar and pledged fresh measures to slow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise and defend local exporters' competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  Kang's remarks, backed up by heavy U.S. dollar purchases executed by the Bank of Korea, were sufficient to drive the U.S. dollar up to an intraday high of 1,135 won from an opening low of KRW1,126. \par \par \uc2  The dollar failed to hang on to its full gains, however, as South Korean exporters and foreign investors took advantage of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall to re-enter the market and buy won. \par \par \uc2  Despite the Seoul government's determination to hold the won down and to preserve the international competitiveness of the country's exports, most analysts believe the authorities will have no option but to permit local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation over the medium term. \par \par \uc2  Allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen, and thus, counter growing inflationary pressure, will be less damaging to the domestic economy than raising interest rates, they argue. \par \par \uc2  "The government does not want to raise interest rates. It still wants to monetize domestic debt," said Steven Xu, regional treasury economist at Standard Chartered Bank in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  Other analysts agree, insisting the won is still set to climb further, with the dollar weakening to levels around KRW1,100 over the next month or so. \par \par \uc2  At Thursday's close, the dollar was quoted at KRW1,130.10, up from KRRW1,126.00 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in the region, the New Taiwan dollar also closed lower as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying continued to support the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  above Wednesday's two-year low at NT$31.550. \par \par \uc2  At the close of trading in Taipei, the U.S. dollar ended at NT$31.616, up from NT$31.605 at Wednesday's finish. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased slightly in uneventful trading. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was little affected by both the crisis at bank Putera, confirmation that Indonesia will seek to reschedule debts owed to commercial lenders, and by the announcement that the budget for the next fiscal year will be based on a projected exchange rate of IDR7,000 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  In thin dealings due to the start of the Muslim holy month of Ramadan, trade in Indonesia was dominated by demand for dollars from local participants afraid market liquidity will deteriorate even further toward the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia the dollar was quoted against the rupiah at IDR7,235, up a touch from IDR7,220 the day before. \par \par \uc2  Dealing in the Thai baht was also lackluster, with the Thai Bankers' Association advising members to cease trading between Dec. 15 and Jan. 15, because of the risk of market illiquidity and settlement problems over the new year period. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asia Thursday, the dollar was quoted against the baht at THB38.6550, up from THB38.5600 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at S$1.6810, up from S$1.6775. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso bucked the regional trend, rising against the U.S. dollar on expatriates' remittances despite concerns over the size of the government's fiscal deficit. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended the Manila session at PHP40.710, down from PHP40.740 at Wednesday's close. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvc908dvs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
