\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 : Won, NT$ Up Despite Govt  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Holland \par \uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 1999\par 10:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- North Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed higher Thursday despite government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  intended to preserve export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped back in quiet trading as market players covered their short positions in the U.S. dollar ahead of the month's end. \par \par \uc2  The release in Seoul of strong economic data for September underlined the power of Korea's economic recovery and helped drive the won higher, as the U.S. dollar dropped to close below the psychologically significant KRW1,200 mark. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere the Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , facing heavy trade and investment inflows, allowed the New Taiwan dollar to continue inching upward toward its highest level in two years. \par \par \uc2  Data released Thursday illustrated the magnitude of the upward pressure on the won, as a booming export sector brought South Korea's current account surplus for the first nine months of the year to $19.16 billion. \par \par \uc2  Although that figure represents a decline of 40% compared with the equivalent period last year, the surplus remains strongly won-supportive. \par \par \uc2  At the same time analysts interpreted the fall favorably for Korea, saying the sharp rise in imports seen in 1999 emphasizes the recovery in the domestic economy. \par \par \uc2  That view was supported by data showing that wholesale and retail sales were up by an impressive 14% in September, compared to the previous year. \par \par \uc2  Despite the fundamental upward pressure on the won, dollar buying by the authorities, which have now built up foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of nearly $65 billion, limited the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains. \par \par \uc2  At the close of trading in Seoul, the U.S. dollar was quoted at KRW1,199.70, compared with KRW1,200.60 at the end of Wednesday's session. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar also edged higher Thursday, with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropping to NT$31.737 in intraday trading, its lowest level since early December 1997. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's losses were limited by heavy buying from Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which over recent months has sought to slow the pace of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation as the domestic economy picks up. \par \par \uc2  By Thursday's close the U.S. dollar had lifted off its earlier low to end the session at NT31.743, down from NT$31.747 the day before. \par \par \uc2  Despite the authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , upward pressure on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considerable and most observers expect the New Taiwan dollar is likely to continue its measured rise over the medium term. \par \par \uc2  Trade and investment inflows remain strong as the Taiwanese economy has largely shrugged off the impact of September's earthquake and the recent easing of tensions with mainland China has only added to foreign investors' enthusiasm. \par \par \uc2  In South East Asia, the rupiah continued to slip in response to increased sniping about the makeup of Indonesia's new cabinet, which just two days previously had been hailed as an artful political compromise. \par \par \uc2  Toward the end of Asian trading, the U.S. dollar had climbed to 6,945 rupiah, up from IDR6,860 the day before. \par \par \uc2  Both the Singapore dollar and the Thai baht also fell back as dealers covered their short positions in the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Late in Asian trading, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at S$1.6691, up from S$1.6682 the previous day. \par \par \uc2  Against the baht, the dollar was at THB38.8150, compared with THB38.7500 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso, too, lost ground, with the dollar remaining well above the PHP40.000 mark, after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Raphael Buenaventura promised a 25-basis-point cut in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key overnight borrowing rate. \par \par \uc2  Although Buenaventura said heavy inflows from Filipinos working overseas should support the peso, pushing the dollar back below the PHP40.000 level, some analysts were skeptical. The pattern of seasonal flows is well-known, they said, and already discounted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  At the close, the U.S. dollar was at PHP40.210, up from PHP40.005 at the end of Wednesday's session. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Holland; 852 2832 2341; tom.holland@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dvas07mi6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
