\par\par \b \uc2  Asia Economy Watch: HK Share Sale Won't End Controversy \b0\par\par\uc2  By James T. Areddy \par \uc2 996 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 1999\par 07:51\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Whether or not they think the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's 1998 stock market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was good policy, analysts give the authorities credit for honoring a pledge to remain a silent shareholder in the city's biggest companies. \par \par \uc2  But the structure of a plan to dispose of shares bought during the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  ensures that questions will continue to frustrate the government's attempts to end the controversy. \par \par \uc2  The government kicked off Monday a glittery marketing campaign for the first share disposals. Under the plan, the government will bundle shares into a fund designed to mirror the benchmark Hang Seng Index. The Tracker Fund of Hong Kong, or TraHK, will be tradable on the local stock market. \par \par \uc2  Pricing details or how much of the portfolio will be offered initially haven't been announced. \par \par \uc2  The government's stock buying in August, 1998, was one of the most remarkable moments of the recent global financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  Officials decided that buying more than US$15 billion of local equity was the only way to combat what Financial Secretary Donald Tsang Yam-kuen Monday called a "merciless and sustained attack on several fronts." \par \par \uc2  The move revived many of the jitters associated with the handover to Chinese rule last year. It came as such a shock that the U.S.-based Heritage Foundation threatened to strip Hong Kong of its mantle as the world's freest economy, and Nobel laureate economists said it was wrong. \par \par \uc2  The portfolio is now worth about US$26 billion, and, to the government's relief, the rebound has been enough to mute much of the initial criticism. \par \par \uc2  As part of the marketing campaign for the new fund, price discounts and other incentives are being offered to local investors. But even as the government touts the shares as affordable, the strong marketing push is likely to drive up demand and, in turn, the price of the fund. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par             Gala Marketing Push\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Monday, Tsang, Hong Kong central banker Joseph Yam Chi-kwong and Ti\par Liang Yang, a well-respected former judge who oversees the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par money, hosted a gala for investment managers and local luminaries to\par announce the Oct. 25 fund sale.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par They showed a slick television advertisement, noting in particular\par that it should appeal to the elderly, unveiled a telephone hotline and\par an Internet site and said marketing leaflets will be distributed\par throughout the territory.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Asked by Dow Jones Newswires whether there is a conflict of interest\par for government officials publicly endorsing one stock offering, but not\par others, Yang replied, "It's a fair question." But he refused to comment\par further.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mark Machin, an executive director of Goldman Sachs (Asia) Ltd. who is\par involved in the mutual fund sale, defended the government's high-powered\par sales effort, saying finance ministers get "involved in marketing around\par the world."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We need to raise public awareness," said Miriam Li, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par fund manager. She likened the officials' public relations efforts to the\par privatization of a state-owned company, such as Australian\par telecommunications company Telstra Corp. (TLS).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "You understand that ours is a special situation," she said, backing\par away from suggesting the companies were ever nationalized.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par              Quarterly Sales Decisions\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But questions being raised about the plan go beyond marketing\par strategy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sales will be an ongoing program and decisions on how much to offer\par will be made quarterly. Li said the government will try to avoid\par swamping the market by selling too much stock.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par With quarterly decisions on how much of the fund to sell, the\par government will likely take into account its other privatization\par efforts, such as the planned initial public offer next year of its\par subway operator.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Kevin Chan, an economist at Nomura International (Hong Kong) Ltd.,\par said the mutual fund plan won't add new liquidity that could make the\par market less volatile. It merely shifts the role of market heavyweight\par away from the government and onto a fund manager.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The first best option is to sell (the shares) directly into the\par market," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Hong Kong affiliate of Boston's State Street Bank and Trust Co. will\par manage the fund. Vincent Duhamel, the affiliate's chief executive,\par acknowledged the sway State Street will hold over the market,\par particularly when there are changes to the make-up of the Hang Seng\par Index that would force the fund to buy or sell stocks that had been\par added or dropped.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This is where the skills of a passive manager come in," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Compared with the government, which has steadfastly avoided\par participating in corporate management since buying the shares last year\par and vows to continue with the policy, State Street will show its face\par more often.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The bank won't shy from voting the shares it controls when proposals\par are put to investors "if we feel it is in the interest of the\par shareholders," Duhamel said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the same time, the system's design will leave the fund manager with\par very little discretion to influence the overall trading environment in\par Hong Kong on most days.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Since the fund is designed to track the 33-stock Hang Seng Index,\par institutional investors will have an arbitrage opportunity to exploit\par any difference between the listed fund's net asset value and the prices\par of underlying individual shares. This should ensure the value of one\par never gets too far from the other.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hong Kong employs the same arbitrage principle in its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  board\par that ensures the Hong Kong dollar trades in lock step with the U.S.\par dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Machin at Goldman Sachs said there is little chance that hedge funds,\par blamed for the 1998 speculation, will come back and profit from the new\par arbitrage opportunities being created.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The hedge funds will not be the market community trading these back\par and forth and keeping the market honest," he said.\par    -By James T. Areddy; james.areddy@dowjones.com; 852.9424.2486\par -0-    12/10/99 05-51G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1172 4255 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020330dvac004hv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
