\par\par \b \uc2  World Markets: Shares End Mixed In Europe; Hong Kong Stocks Fall 1% \b0\par\par\uc2 965 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 1999\par 06:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Shares in Europe closed mixed Monday, with trading volume thin because of holidays in the U.S. and Japan. Stock markets in Brazil and Mexico closed higher, but markets in Argentina and Canada were closed Monday. Asian stock markets ended mostly lower without Tokyo's guidance, with Hong Kong's key index falling about 1%. \par \par \uc2  With Tokyo financial markets closed and the Columbus Day holiday closing the U.S. Treasurys market, European investors had little inspiration despite some early modest gains on Wall Street. The Dow Jones Industrials went on to close down less than two points, but the Nasdaq Composite climbed more than 29 points to a record high. \par \par \uc2  On the European economic front, gross domestic product in the 11-country euro zone grew 0.5% in the second quarter of 1999, compared with the first quarter, the European Union statistical agency Eurostat said. That was an upward revision from the 0.3% growth previously reported. \par \par \uc2  Second-quarter GDP was up 1.6% compared with the second quarter of 1998, primarily because of a strong pickup in second-quarter exports, Eurostat said. \par \par \uc2  In London, the FT-SE 100 index closed up 35.4 points, or 0.6%, at 6234.8, after slipping just one point on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Pharmaceutical shares rose on merger speculation reignited by talk that SmithKline Beecham chief executive Jan Leschly is to retire early. SmithKline rose 38.5 pence, or 5%, to 813 pence, and Glaxo Wellcome rose 70 pence, or 4%, to 1,801 pence, despite SmithKline's denial that Leschly is leaving. Leschly was seen as the main obstacle to a deal between the pharmaceutical companies after talks broke down last year. \par \par \uc2  Frankfurt's Xetra DAX index of 30 blue chips slipped 4.76 points, or 0.1%, at 5414.50, after ending little changed Friday. \par \par \uc2  In Paris, the CAC-40 index of most actively traded French stocks edged up just 0.42 point to 4722.35 after Friday's 0.4% decline. Interest-rate concerns kept a lid on trading. \par \par \uc2  "We just can't seem to get away from these rate-hike fears, whether it's in the U.S. or in Europe," said Richard Morisseau, a trader at Paris brokerage Meeschaert-Rousselle. "It's making investors very wary and they're staying well out of the market." \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in Europe, Zurich's Swiss Market index rose 13.50 points, or 0.2%; Madrid's IBEX-35 index ended up 37.3 points, or 0.4%; Amsterdam's AEX index rose 4.93 points, or 0.9%, and Stockholm's Swedish General index rose 19.16 points, or 0.4%. But Milan's MIBtel index declined 49 points, or 0.2%. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Stoxx Index of 614 European companies rose 0.78 points to close at 313.15. The Dow Jones Euro Stoxx Index, which tracks 310 companies in countries that joined the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , closed up 0.53 at 327.75. \par \par \uc2  In Moscow, the benchmark RTS Index rallied 3.88 points, or 4.2%, to end at 97.19. Trading volume increased to $13.2 million from $10.5 million Friday. \par \par \uc2  Russian traders brushed off news about President Boris Yeltsin's brief weekend hospital stay, connected with a bout of flu. Yeltsin has been plagued with health problems since his re-election in 1996, and news of his hospitalization has become a routine event in Russia. \par \par \uc2  Turning to Latin America, Brazil's Bovespa index rose 25.02 points, or 0.2%, to close at 11466.35 after climbing 1.3% Friday. Trading was at a near standstill ahead of Tuesday's national holiday. \par \par \uc2  "There's not much to say about the market because there aren't any flows. Most people are taking advantage of Tuesday's holiday to have a four-day weekend," said a trader at Unibanco in Sao Paulo. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the Brazilian market is anxiously awaiting Thursday's scheduled announcement by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  about a package of measures to help ease consumer lending rates. Optimism about the announcement should give the market support when trading resumes on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Mexican stocks finished higher for a fourth session. The IPC index climbed 48.06 points, or 0.9%, to finish at 5331.02 after jumping 1.9% on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that, despite a slowdown in trading, there was significant interest from foreign investors in Mexican equities. They noted that expectations of strong third-quarter earnings were attracting foreign investors into Mexican issues, many of which are at bargain-basement prices resulting from the market's recent slump. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in Asia, Hong Kong's Hang Seng index fell 119.70 points, or 0.9%, to close at 12992.72, after slipping 0.78 point Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong government said it will begin retail sales of its long-awaited Hong Kong Index Fund, which will track the blue-chip Hang Seng index. The mutual-fund listing is the government's first attempt to divest itself of shareholdings amassed in a mammoth market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year that are worth more than 200 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars (US$25.74 billion). \par \par \uc2  Banking and property stocks, which make up the bulk of the government shares, saw more selling as some investors unloaded them in anticipation that the new fund will allow investors to buy the shares at a 5% to 9% discount to current market prices, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Most other Asian stock markets closed lower, with Thailand's SET Index falling 1.1% and South Korea's Kospi ending down 0.6%. But Taiwan's Weighted Price Index rose 0.7%, boosted by stength in some banking and technology shares. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020330dvab003gp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
