\par\par \b \uc2  Obscure Rule Changes Saved HK Dlr - Nobel Prize Winner \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jon E. Hilsenrath \par \uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 1999\par 11:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Ten months after the Hong Kong government's decision to pour $15 billion into its own stock market, the Hong Kong dollar is a picture of stability and stock prices are still rising. So the controversial approach to supporting the beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a success, right? \par \par \uc2  Not quite, says Merton Miller, a Nobel prize winning economist with the University of Chicago, who was a vocal critic of the government's stock market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last September. \par \par \uc2  Miller, who is visiting \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday, says Hong Kong officials instead can credit two relatively obscure, technical changes in the way the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  manages its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Those changes, he says, helped preserve the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the U.S. dollar at HK$7.8 for every US$1, a policy in place since 1983 that came under tremendous stress during the financial turmoil of 1997 and 1998. \par \par \uc2  The first, announced shortly after the September 1998 stock market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was a promise to all of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's banks that the HKMA would convert Hong Kong dollars into U.S. dollars at a rate of HK$7.75 to US$1 for the next six months. The promise removed concerns among the banks that they would face losses if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was devalued, and thus eased the run on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par         Free-Market Image Tarnished By \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The second was a commitment to allow banks to borrow from the HKMA at\par "non-prohibitive" interest rates if they became short of funds. This\par policy was in direct contrast to its policy in October 1997, when HKMA\par tried to punish banks for lending to hedge funds by threatening to\par charge high interest rates if these banks came to the HKMA for\par short-term cash.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par When the government made that threat in 1997, interest rates soared as\par banks scrambled for other sources of cash, and the soaring interest\par rates helped to knock the stock market lower. But with the government's\par promise to provide funds to the banks at more reasonable rates in 1998,\par Miller says, the punishing effect of high interest rates quickly\par subsided.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is finally doing the right thing,"\par he says.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The government argued that the stock market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was necessary\par to keep hedge funds from selling shares and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the same\par time. But Miller contends, as he did last year, that all \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par did was tarnish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Hong Kong\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's image as a free market. And because of that,\par this university professor gives the Hong Kong government a mixed grade\par for its handling of the crisis.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "They flunked the mid-term," he says of the decision by policy makers\par to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the stock market. "The problem that I see in Hong Kong\par is they're not willing anymore to let the markets tell them what the\par score is."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 05-07-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0947GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv75050b5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
