\par\par \b \uc2  HK Dollar Steady Midday, Seen Testing Weaker In Late Trading \b0\par\par\uc2 337 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 1999\par 06:51\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar is nearly steady midday Thursday, but U.S. dollar-buying by domestic banks is expected to drive it weaker in afternoon trading, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at HK$7.7515 at 0430 GMT (12:30 a.m. EDT), nearly flat from late Wednesday's HK$7.7516 per dollar level. Forward premiums are higher due to expectations of a weakening spot rate and because of stock market volatility. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a U.S. bank said domestic banks were seen buying the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  early in transactions just under US$100 million each. \par \par \uc2  They're doing so because they're trying to test the U.S. dollar's upside. They believe its bottom is HK$7.7495-HK$7.7500 and now want to see how close they can get to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate of HK$7.7537 before taking profits. \par \par \uc2  "I think the market will like to test the upside," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  He noted that strong two-way activity has so far kept the Hong Kong dollar bottled in a tight range. But Hong Kong dollar buying should ease late Thursday, he said, and that will lead a test of HK$7.7520. \par \par \uc2  He said he's not seeing any capital outflow from Hong Kong's markets. \par \par \uc2  Most forward premiums are higher, due to volatility in the stock market and a pickup in hedging due to some concerns over the expectations of a Hong Kong dollar weakening. The one-month contract is even with the spot exchange rate, compared with a three-point discount late Wednesday. The three-month premium is 35 points, up a touch from 28 points. The six-month premium is 200 points, up from 170, and the one-year premium is 740 points, up from 685 points. \par \par \uc2  -By Adam Najberg; (852)2802-7002; naj@asiaonline.net \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 06-05-99 \par \par \uc2  0451GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv5603g2s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
