\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong Banking Cartel Cuts Rates For 8th Time In 14 Months \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sean Kennedy, Staff Reporter \par \uc2 734 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 1999\par 16:49\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- The local rate-setting cartel cut deposit rates for the eighth time in 14 months Friday, but bankers and analysts said the move probably won't cause a surge in Hong Kong's stock market next week. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong Association of Banks cut deposit rates one-quarter point to 3.5% at its weekly meeting, its second rate cut this month. \par \par \uc2  The rate cut probably will be reflected Monday in the activities of the few market participants who said they hadn't expected a rate policy move until at least the middle of May. But the probability of a rate cut had grown throughout the week, and most dealers and brokers said they had already factored such a move into their buying and selling strategies. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar may face some initial pressure and interbank rates will definitely go down. If the stock market resumes its upward course, however, the effect on the Hong Kong dollar will be mitigated by a continued steady inflow of overseas funds into Hong Kong markets, bankers and analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The rate-setting cartel as much as signaled that the cut was its last unilateral cut before it goes back to following moves by the U.S. Federal Reserve. Hong Kong tends to follow the Fed's moves because the Hong Kong dollar is pegged to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The territory's major banks immediately followed the cartel's lead Friday, announcing matching percentage cuts in their individual prime-lending rates. Most banks' best lending rates dropped to 8.25% from 8.5%. \par \par \uc2  Liu Jinbao, chairman of the Hong Kong Associatin of Bankers, told reporters that the cut was justified, since the continued wide spread between U.S. and Hong Kong interest rates have been acting as a drag on the domestic economy. \par \par \uc2  "Hong Kong's real interest rates are still very high, which is unfavorable to Hong Kong's economic recovery," Liu said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said it is hard to see how Friday's rate cut could further boost a stock market that has rallied 22% this month, and is looking a touch overvalued. \par \par \uc2  In fact, Louie Shum, associate director of Emperor Securities Ltd., predicted that the Hang Seng Index will be hit by profit-taking by late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Property stocks probably will be the only major beneficiaries of the rate cut, said Alex Wong, research manager at OSK Asia Securities Ltd. \par \par \uc2  The stock market is trading at a premium now, and the only way it will get a major boost is through some external good news, Wong said. \par \par \uc2  The story is likely to be the same in the money market, traders said, where HKAB has merely played "catch-up" with the wholesale market. \par \par \uc2  "It (HKAB) is just ratifying what the market has done already with interbank rates," said Andrew Fung, treasurer of the Hong Kong branch of the Commonwealth Bank of Australia. Fung noted that interbank rates have eased steadily in recent weeks on expectations of another deposit rate cut. \par \par \uc2  Mike Powell, head of interest rates with HSBC Markets, a unit of HSBC Holdings PLC., said the spot Hong Kong dollar will weaken, but not significantly. \par \par \uc2  Local interbank rates are already below their U.S. counterparts in some cases, he said. "You can hold U.S. dollars and get a slight premium," Powell said, noting that inflows of hot cash in recent weeks have helped offset the effect of arbitraging into U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  At International Bank of Asia Hong Kong, Treasury Chief Davy Kwan said the weakening of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rates could lead money market investors to switch to higher-yielding U.S. dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar is likely to move closer to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily moving \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, he said. That rate - at HK$7.7501 April 1 - is moving one point per day until it hits the official peg rate of HK$7.80 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I do not think the Hong Kong dollar will weaken much because of the\par much-improved environment in Hong Kong," Kwan said. "There will be some\par more hot money, enough to balance a drop."\par   - Sean Kennedy; (852)2802-7002; sean.kennedy@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Copyright (c) 1999 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All Rights Reserved.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1154 55128 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329dv4u02eqd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
