\par\par \b \uc2  HK Rate Cut Not Seen Spurring -3: Some Caught By Surprise \b0\par\par\uc2 350 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 1999\par 12:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  International Bank of Asia Hong Kong Treasury Chief Davy Kwan said the weakening of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rates could lead money market investors to switch to higher-yielding U.S. dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  The expected weakening effect on the Hong Kong dollar will be somewhat mitigated, though, by a continued inflow of overseas money headed for the stock market because of lower interest rates, he said. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar is likely to move closer to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily moving \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, he said. That rate - at HK$7.7501 April 1 - is moving one point per day until it hits the official peg rate of HK$7.80 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "I do not think the Hong Kong dollar will weaken much because of the much-improved environment in Hong Kong," Kwan said. "There will be some more hot money, enough to balance a drop." \par \par \uc2  While many had expected a rate cut in the near future, and though talk of one gathered steam throughout the week, Friday's decision may have caught some off-guard. That surprise will be reflected in market activity Monday and beyond, said Kwan of the International Bank of Asia Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  "On the interbank market, I think it was slightly unexpected. It came a little bit too early. Most expected a cut only in mid-May or end-May," he said. "I expect interbank rates will drop by maybe 10 (basis points) to 15 basis points." \par \par \uc2  And economists said the rate cut isn't a cure-all for Hong Kong's still-fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  "Real interest rates are still high," said Core Pacific - Yamaichi International (HK) Ltd. economist Andy Wong. He noted that real interest rates are still just below 11% - due largely to deflation of a year-on-year -2.6% as of March. \par \par \uc2  Wong said he doesn't see any sign of the economy recovering until late this year. \par \par \uc2  -by Sean Kennedy; (852)2802-7002; sean.kennedy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 30-04-99 \par \par \uc2  1053GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv4u03bfr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
